\id REV Veni Bible Translation (VBT), en, v27
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Revelation
\toc1 Revelation
\toc2 Revelation
\toc3 Rev
\mt1 Revelation
\c 1
\s1 The revelation of Jesus Christ
\p \v 1 The revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave Him to show His servants the things that must soon happen; and He made it known by signs, sending it through His angel to His servant John,\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Gr. δούλοις, lit. “slaves”. “Made it known by signs”: Gr. ἐσήμανεν, a verb related to σημεῖον, “sign”; the book communicates through symbols.\f* \v 2 who testified to the Word of God and the Testimony of Jesus Christ, all that he saw.\f + \fr 1:2 \ft The μαρτυρ- family is always rendered testify / testimony / Witness (cf. 12:17; 19:10).\f* \v 3 Blessed is the one who reads, and those who hear the words of the prophecy and keep what is written in it, for the time is near.\f + \fr 1:3 \ft A single “blessed” (μακάριος) covers the reader, in the singular (the one who reads aloud in the assembly), and a single group that hears and keeps; one article governs both participles. τηρέω = “keep” throughout the book (cf. 12:17; 14:12).\f*
\s1 Grace and peace to the seven churches
\q1 \v 4 John, to the seven churches that are in Asia:
\q1 Grace to you and peace
\q1 from Him Who Is, and Who Was, and Who Is Coming,
\q1 and from the Seven Spirits who are before His throne,\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Gr. ἀπὸ ὁ ὤν: the divine name stays in the nominative after the preposition, as an indeclinable name (cf. Ex 3:14). “Who Is Coming”: present tense (ὁ ἐρχόμενος), the same verb as “He is coming” in 1:7. “Seven Spirits”: the capital follows their place in the greeting, between Him Who Is and Jesus Christ, as the source of grace and peace; alternative reading: seven angelic spirits (cf. 8:2).\f*
\q1 \v 5 and from Jesus Christ, the faithful Witness, the Firstborn of the dead, and the Ruler of the kings of the earth.
\q1 To Him who loves us
\q1 and freed us from our sins by His blood,\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Variants: the Textus Receptus reads “loved us”; it and the Byzantine text read “washed us” (λούσαντι). The SBLGNT is followed, with Papyrus 18 and codices Sinaiticus, Alexandrinus and Ephraemi: “loves us” (ἀγαπῶντι) and “freed us” (λύσαντι).\f*
\q1 \v 6 and made us a kingdom, priests to His God and Father,
\q1 to Him be the glory and the dominion forever and ever. Amen.\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “kings and priests”; the SBLGNT, “a kingdom, priests” (cf. Ex 19:6). “Forever and ever” (εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, lit. “to the ages of the ages”): the formula is rendered the same way throughout the book.\f*
\q1 \v 7 Behold, He is coming with the clouds,
\q1 and every eye will see Him,
\q1 and also those who pierced Him;
\q1 and all the tribes of the earth will mourn over Him.
\q1 Yes, amen.\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Cf. Dan 7:13; Zech 12:10. “Yes, amen”: Gr. ναί (Greek) + ἀμήν (Hebrew), a double affirmation.\f*
\q1 \v 8 I am the Alpha and the Omega, says the Lord God,
\q1 He Who Is, and Who Was, and Who Is Coming, the Almighty.\f + \fr 1:8 \ft “Almighty”: Gr. παντοκράτωρ, which in the Septuagint usually renders “LORD of Hosts” (e.g. Amos 4:13) and, in Job, “Shaddai”. Variant: the Textus Receptus adds “the Beginning and the End” and reads “says the Lord”, without “God”.\f*
\s1 Christ among the lampstands
\p \v 9 I, John, your brother and companion in the tribulation and the kingdom and the perseverance in Jesus, was on the island called Patmos because of the Word of God and the Testimony of Jesus.\f + \fr 1:9 \ft “Was” renders ἐγενόμην, a verb repeated in 1:9, 1:10 and 1:18. ὑπομονή = “perseverance” (cf. 14:12). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “in Christ Jesus”, and the Textus Receptus “of Jesus Christ”; both read “the Testimony of Jesus Christ”.\f* \v 10 I was in the Spirit on the Lord’s day, and I heard behind me a loud voice like a trumpet,\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Gr. ἐν τῇ κυριακῇ ἡμέρᾳ, with the adjective κυριακός, “belonging to the Lord”; the text does not name the day. Cf. Ex 20:10; Isa 58:13; Mark 2:28.\f* \v 11 saying: What you see, write in a book, and send it to the seven churches: to Ephesus, and to Smyrna, and to Pergamum, and to Thyatira, and to Sardis, and to Philadelphia, and to Laodicea.\f + \fr 1:11 \ft Gr. βιβλίον, a scroll; “book” is kept for consistency with 5:1ff. Variant: the Textus Receptus begins the speech with “I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last” and adds “that are in Asia”.\f*
\p \v 12 And I turned to see the voice that was speaking with me, and when I turned I saw seven golden lampstands,\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Gr. λυχνία; cf. the lampstand of the sanctuary, Ex 25:31–37.\f* \v 13 and in the midst of the lampstands One like a Son of Man, clothed in a robe reaching to the feet and girded at the chest with a golden sash.\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Cf. Dan 7:13; “a Son of Man”: Gr. υἱὸν ἀνθρώπου, without the article, as in Daniel. “Robe reaching to the feet”: Gr. ποδήρη, the term for the high priest’s garments in the Septuagint (Ex 28:4; 28:31 [LXX 28:27]; cf. Zech 3:4). The capital follows the self-identification in 1:17–18. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “the seven lampstands”.\f*
\q1 \v 14 His head and hair were white like white wool, like snow;
\q1 and His eyes were like a flame of fire;\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Cf. Dan 7:9 (the Ancient of Days).\f*
\q1 \v 15 His feet were like gleaming bronze, as though refined in a furnace;
\q1 and His voice was like the voice of many waters.\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Gr. χαλκολίβανον, a metal of uncertain identification. “Refined”: the same verb as in 3:18 (πυρόω). Cf. Ezek 43:2.\f*
\q1 \v 16 In His right hand He held seven stars,
\q1 and out of His mouth came a sharp two-edged sword;
\q1 and His face was like the sun when it shines in its strength.\f + \fr 1:16 \ft Cf. Isa 49:2; Judg 5:31.\f*
\p \v 17 When I saw Him, I fell at His feet as one dead. Then He laid His right hand on me, saying: Do not fear; I am the First and the Last,\f + \fr 1:17 \ft “Right hand”: Gr. τὴν δεξιάν, “the right”, without the word “hand” (also in 1:20); in 1:16 the Greek has “right hand”. Variant: the Textus Receptus adds “hand” here. Cf. Isa 44:6; 48:12.\f* \v 18 and the Living One. I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever, and I have the keys of death and of the grave.\f + \fr 1:18 \ft “The grave”: Gr. ᾅδης (Hades), equivalent to Heb. sheol (cf. Hos 13:14). Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus add “Amen” after “forever and ever”; the Textus Receptus reads “of the grave and of death”.\f* \v 19 Write, therefore, the things that you saw, and the things that are, and the things that are about to happen after these things.\f + \fr 1:19 \ft Or: “the things that you saw, that is, the things that are and the things that are about to happen after these things”.\f* \v 20 As for the mystery of the seven stars that you saw in My right hand, and of the seven golden lampstands: the seven stars are the angels of the seven churches, and the seven lampstands are the seven churches.\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Gr. ἄγγελοι, or “messengers”.\f*
\c 2
\s1 To the church in Ephesus
\q1 \v 1 To the angel of the church in Ephesus write:
\q1 Thus says He who holds the seven stars in His right hand,
\q1 He who walks in the midst of the seven golden lampstands:\f + \fr 2:1 \ft “Angel”: Gr. ἄγγελος, or “messenger” (cf. 1:20). “Thus says”: Gr. τάδε λέγει, the prophetic formula of the Septuagint (“Thus says the LORD”), repeated in all seven letters. “Holds”: Gr. κρατῶν, from κρατέω, “to hold firmly”.\f*
\p \v 2 I know your works, your toil and your perseverance, and that you cannot bear those who are evil; you have tested those who call themselves apostles, and are not, and have found them liars;\f + \fr 2:2 \ft “Toil”: Gr. κόπος, “wearying toil”; the related verb appears in 2:3 (“have not grown weary”).\f* \v 3 and you have perseverance, and have borne up for My name’s sake, and have not grown weary. \v 4 But I have this against you: you have left your first love. \v 5 Remember, then, from where you have fallen, and repent, and do the first works; and if not, I am coming to you and will move your lampstand from its place, unless you repent.\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Gr. κινήσω, “move, remove from its place”; the lampstand is the church (1:20). “Have fallen”: perfect (πέπτωκας), a fall whose state remains. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus add “quickly” after “I am coming to you”.\f* \v 6 But this you have: you hate the works of the Nicolaitans, which I also hate.\f + \fr 2:6 \ft The Nicolaitans are mentioned only in Revelation (cf. 2:15).\f*
\p \v 7 Whoever has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches. To the one who conquers I will give to eat from the tree of life, which is in the paradise of God.\f + \fr 2:7 \ft “To the one who conquers” (νικῶντι): νικάω = “conquer” in all seven letters. Gr. ξύλον, “wood, tree”, the term of Gen 2:9 (Septuagint), also used in the New Testament for the cross (Acts 5:30); cf. 22:2, 14. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “the paradise of My God”; the Textus Receptus, “in the midst of the paradise of God”.\f*
\s1 To the church in Smyrna
\q1 \v 8 And to the angel of the church in Smyrna write:
\q1 Thus says the First and the Last,
\q1 who was dead and came to life:\f + \fr 2:8 \ft “Was dead”: Gr. ἐγένετο νεκρός, as in 1:18 (“I was dead”). “Came to life”: Gr. ἔζησεν, the same verb as in 20:4–5.\f*
\p \v 9 I know your tribulation and your poverty (but you are rich), and the blasphemy of those who say they are Jews, and are not, but are a synagogue of Satan.\f + \fr 2:9 \ft Or “slander” (Gr. βλασφημία). The expression does not refer to the Jewish people as such, but to those who, according to the text itself, “say they are Jews, and are not” (cf. 3:9; Rom 2:28–29). Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus begin “I know your works, and your tribulation”.\f* \v 10 Do not fear anything that you are about to suffer. Behold, the devil is about to cast some of you into prison, that you may be tested, and you will have tribulation for ten days. Be faithful until death, and I will give you the crown of life.\f + \fr 2:10 \ft “Tested”: Gr. πειρασθῆτε, the same verb as in 2:2 (“you have tested”). “Ten days”: cf. Dan 1:12, 14. “Crown”: Gr. στέφανος, the victor’s wreath, distinct from διάδημα, “diadem” (12:3; 13:1; 19:12).\f*
\p \v 11 Whoever has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches. The one who conquers will by no means be harmed by the second death.\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Gr. οὐ μή, an emphatic negation. Second death: cf. 20:6, 14; 21:8.\f*
\s1 To the church in Pergamum
\q1 \v 12 And to the angel of the church in Pergamum write:
\q1 Thus says He who has the sharp two-edged sword:
\p \v 13 I know where you dwell: where the throne of Satan is. And you hold fast My name, and you did not deny My faith, even in the days of Antipas, My witness, My faithful one, who was killed among you, where Satan dwells.\f + \fr 2:13 \ft “Hold fast”: Gr. κρατέω, the same verb as in 2:1 (“holds”), 2:14–15 (“hold”) and 2:25 (“hold fast”). “My faith”: or “faith in Me” (cf. 14:12). “Witness”: Gr. μάρτυς, the same word as in 1:5, which later gave “martyr”. “Dwell… dwells”: the same verb (κατοικέω). Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus begin “I know your works and where you dwell”.\f* \v 14 But I have a few things against you: you have there those who hold the teaching of Balaam, who taught Balak to cast a stumbling block before the sons of Israel, so that they would eat food sacrificed to idols and prostitute themselves.\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Cf. Num 25:1–3; 31:16. “Teaching… taught”: Gr. διδαχή / ἐδίδασκεν. “Stumbling block”: Gr. σκάνδαλον. “Prostitute themselves”: Gr. πορνεύω; in Num 25:1 the Hebrew uses זָנָה‎, “to prostitute oneself”, linked to idolatry; in Revelation the term joins sexual and spiritual unfaithfulness (cf. 17:1–2).\f* \v 15 So you also have those who likewise hold the teaching of the Nicolaitans.\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “which I hate” in place of “likewise”.\f* \v 16 Repent, then; and if not, I am coming to you soon and will make war against them with the sword of My mouth.\f + \fr 2:16 \ft “Soon”: Gr. ταχύ (cf. 3:11; 22:7, 12, 20). “Make war”: Gr. πολεμέω (cf. 12:7; 17:14; 19:11).\f*
\p \v 17 Whoever has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches. To the one who conquers I will give some of the hidden manna, and I will give him a white pebble, and on the pebble a new name written, which no one knows except the one who receives it.\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Hidden manna: cf. Ex 16:33–34. “Pebble”: Gr. ψῆφος, a small stone used in voting and as a token of admission. “New”: Gr. καινός (cf. 3:12; 21:1, 5). “Knows”: the same verb as “I know” (οἶδα).\f*
\s1 To the church in Thyatira
\q1 \v 18 And to the angel of the church in Thyatira write:
\q1 Thus says the Son of God,
\q1 who has His eyes like a flame of fire
\q1 and His feet like gleaming bronze:\f + \fr 2:18 \ft Cf. 1:14–15.\f*
\p \v 19 I know your works, and your love, and faith, and service, and perseverance; and your last works are more than the first.\f + \fr 2:19 \ft Or: “I know your works, that is, your love, and faith…”.\f* \v 20 But I have this against you: you tolerate the woman Jezebel, who calls herself a prophetess, and she teaches and deceives My servants to prostitute themselves and to eat food sacrificed to idols.\f + \fr 2:20 \ft “Tolerate”: Gr. ἀφεῖς, the same verb as in 2:4 (“have left”). “Woman”: Gr. γυνή, also “wife”; variant: the Byzantine text reads “your wife Jezebel”, and the Textus Receptus “I have a few things against you”. “Deceives”: Gr. πλανάω (cf. 12:9; 13:14; 20:3, 8, 10). Cf. 1 Kgs 16:31.\f* \v 21 And I gave her time to repent, and she is not willing to repent of her prostitution. \v 22 Behold, I am casting her onto a bed, and those who commit adultery with her into great tribulation, unless they repent of her works;\f + \fr 2:22 \ft “Bed”: Gr. κλίνη, also a sickbed. “Great tribulation”: cf. 7:14. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “of their works”.\f* \v 23 and I will kill her children with pestilence; and all the churches will know that I am He who searches minds and hearts, and I will give to each one of you according to your works.\f + \fr 2:23 \ft “With pestilence”: Gr. ἐν θανάτῳ, lit. “with death”; in the Septuagint θάνατος often renders Heb. דֶּבֶר‎, “pestilence” (cf. 6:8; 18:8; Ezek 5:12; 33:27). “Minds”: Gr. νεφροί, lit. “kidneys”, in Hebrew thought the seat of the emotions and conscience (Jer 17:10, Heb. כְּלָיוֹת‎).\f* \v 24 But to you I say, to the remnant in Thyatira, all who do not have this teaching, who have not known the depths of Satan, as they say: I do not cast on you any other burden;\f + \fr 2:24 \ft “Remnant”: Gr. λοιποί, “those who remain”; the same word as in 12:17. Where it designates other groups (9:20; 19:21; 20:5), it is rendered “the rest”. “Burden”: Gr. βάρος, cf. Acts 15:28–29. “Depths”: cf. 1 Cor 2:10.\f* \v 25 only, what you have, hold fast until I come.
\p \v 26 The one who conquers and keeps My works until the end, to him I will give authority over the nations,\f + \fr 2:26 \ft “Keeps”: Gr. τηρέω (cf. 1:3). “Nations”: Gr. ἔθνη.\f* \v 27 and he will shepherd them with a rod of iron, as clay vessels are shattered,\f + \fr 2:27 \ft Cf. Ps 2:8–9. “Shepherd”: Gr. ποιμαίνω, “to tend as a shepherd”, as in the Septuagint of Ps 2:9; the Hebrew reads “you will break” (תְּרֹעֵם‎). Cf. 7:17; 12:5; 19:15.\f* \v 28 even as I also have received from My Father; and I will give him the morning star.\f + \fr 2:28 \ft Cf. 22:16.\f*
\p \v 29 Whoever has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches.
\c 3
\s1 To the church in Sardis
\q1 \v 1 And to the angel of the church in Sardis write:
\q1 Thus says He who has the Seven Spirits of God
\q1 and the seven stars:
\p I know your works, that you have a name of being alive, and you are dead.\f + \fr 3:1 \ft “Seven Spirits”: cf. 1:4. “A name of being alive”: Gr. ὄνομα ἔχεις ὅτι ζῇς, lit. “a name that you live”; “name”, that is, reputation; the word recurs in 3:4–5.\f* \v 2 Be watchful, and strengthen the things that remain, which were about to die; for I have not found your works complete before My God.\f + \fr 3:2 \ft “Be watchful”: Gr. γίνου γρηγορῶν, like “Be faithful” (2:10); γρηγορέω = “watch” (3:3; 16:15). “The things that remain”: Gr. τὰ λοιπά (plural). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “which you were about to throw away” (ἔμελλες ἀποβάλλειν); the Textus Receptus reads “which is about to die” (μέλλει, singular and present) and “before God”, without “My”.\f* \v 3 Remember, then, how you have received and heard, and keep it, and repent. If, then, you do not watch, I will come like a thief, and you will by no means know at what hour I will come upon you.\f + \fr 3:3 \ft “Like a thief”: cf. 16:15; Matt 24:43; 1 Thess 5:2. “I will come”: Gr. ἥξω, as in 2:25 (“until I come”). “By no means”: Gr. οὐ μή, an emphatic negation. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “I will come upon you like a thief”.\f* \v 4 But you have a few names in Sardis who have not defiled their garments, and they will walk with Me in white, for they are worthy.\f + \fr 3:4 \ft “Names”: that is, persons (cf. Acts 1:15); a wordplay with “name” in 3:1 and 3:5. “Garments”: Gr. ἱμάτια (cf. 3:5, 18; 16:15). “Defiled”: Gr. μολύνω (cf. 14:4).\f*
\p \v 5 The one who conquers will thus be clothed in white garments, and I will by no means blot out his name from the book of life, and I will confess his name before My Father and before His angels.\f + \fr 3:5 \ft “Book of life”: cf. Ex 32:32–33; Dan 12:1; Phil 4:3; Rev 13:8; 20:12, 15. “Blot out”: Gr. ἐξαλείφω, the same verb as in 7:17 and 21:4 (“wipe away”). Cf. Matt 10:32. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “this one will be clothed” (οὗτος) in place of “will thus be clothed” (οὕτως).\f* \v 6 Whoever has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches.
\s1 To the church in Philadelphia
\q1 \v 7 And to the angel of the church in Philadelphia write:
\q1 Thus says the Holy One, the True One,
\q1 He who has the key of David,
\q1 who opens, and no one will shut,
\q1 and shuts, and no one opens:\f + \fr 3:7 \ft Cf. Isa 22:22. “True One”: Gr. ἀληθινός (cf. 3:14; 19:11). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “and no one shuts, and shuts, and no one opens” (κλείει, present, in both places); the Byzantine text, “and no one will shut it except the one who opens, and no one will open”.\f*
\p \v 8 I know your works (behold, I have set before you an open door, which no one is able to shut), that you have little power, and you have kept My word, and have not denied My name.\f + \fr 3:8 \ft “Set”: Gr. δέδωκα, lit. “I have given”. “That you have”: or “because you have”. “Power”: Gr. δύναμις (cf. 4:11; 5:12).\f* \v 9 Behold, I am handing over some of the synagogue of Satan, of those who say they are Jews, and are not, but lie; behold, I will make them come and bow down before your feet, and know that I loved you.\f + \fr 3:9 \ft “Handing over”: Gr. διδῶ, lit. “I give”; the Greek sentence breaks off and starts again (“behold, I will make them…”). “Bow down”: Gr. προσκυνέω, the verb rendered “worship” when the object is God (cf. 4:10; 14:7); here, homage (cf. Isa 60:14 Heb.; 45:14; 49:23). On “synagogue of Satan”, cf. 2:9.\f* \v 10 Because you have kept the word of My perseverance, I also will keep you from the hour of testing that is about to come upon the whole world, to test those who dwell on the earth.\f + \fr 3:10 \ft “Kept… keep”: the same verb (τηρέω). “The word of My perseverance”: or “My word about perseverance”. “Testing… test”: Gr. πειρασμός / πειράζω (cf. πειράζω in 2:2, 10). “The whole world”: Gr. οἰκουμένη ὅλη, lit. “the whole inhabited world” (cf. 12:9). “Those who dwell on the earth”: a recurring formula (cf. 6:10; 13:8).\f* \v 11 I am coming soon; hold fast what you have, so that no one takes your crown.\f + \fr 3:11 \ft “Soon”: Gr. ταχύ (cf. 2:16). “Hold fast”: Gr. κρατέω (cf. 2:25). “Crown”: Gr. στέφανος (cf. 2:10). Variant: the Textus Receptus begins “Behold, I am coming soon”.\f*
\p \v 12 The one who conquers, I will make him a pillar in the sanctuary of My God, and he will by no means go out of it again; and I will write on him the name of My God, and the name of the city of My God, the new Jerusalem, which comes down out of heaven from My God, and My new name.\f + \fr 3:12 \ft “Sanctuary”: Gr. ναός, the inner part of the temple, distinct from ἱερόν, the whole temple complex; in Revelation, always ναός (cf. 7:15; 11:19; 15:5). “New Jerusalem”: cf. 21:2. “New name”: cf. 2:17; Isa 62:2.\f* \v 13 Whoever has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches.
\s1 To the church in Laodicea
\q1 \v 14 And to the angel of the church in Laodicea write:
\q1 Thus says the Amen,
\q1 the faithful and true Witness,
\q1 the Beginning of the creation of God:\f + \fr 3:14 \ft “The Amen”: cf. Isa 65:16 (Heb. “the God of amen”; Septuagint “the true God”). “Beginning”: Gr. ἀρχή, “beginning, origin” or “ruler”; the same term used as a title in 21:6 and 22:13 (“the Beginning and the End”). Cf. John 1:3; Col 1:15–18.\f*
\p \v 15 I know your works, that you are neither cold nor hot. Would that you were cold or hot!\f + \fr 3:15 \ft “Hot”: Gr. ζεστός, lit. “boiling”.\f* \v 16 So, because you are lukewarm, and neither hot nor cold, I am about to vomit you out of My mouth.\f + \fr 3:16 \ft “Am about to”: Gr. μέλλω (cf. 1:19; 2:10).\f* \v 17 Because you say: I am rich, and have grown rich, and have need of nothing; and you do not know that it is you who are wretched, and pitiable, and poor, and blind, and naked,\f + \fr 3:17 \ft “Have grown rich”: perfect (πεπλούτηκα), “I have become rich” (a state that remains).\f* \v 18 I counsel you to buy from Me gold refined by fire, so that you may be rich; and white garments, so that you may clothe yourself and the shame of your nakedness may not be made manifest; and eye salve to anoint your eyes, so that you may see.\f + \fr 3:18 \ft “Refined”: Gr. πυρόω (cf. 1:15). “Clothe yourself”: Gr. περιβάλλω, the same verb as in 3:5 (“clothed”).\f* \v 19 As for Me, all whom I love I rebuke and discipline; be zealous, then, and repent.\f + \fr 3:19 \ft “Love”: Gr. φιλέω. Cf. Prov 3:12 (Septuagint, with the same verb “rebuke”).\f* \v 20 Behold, I stand at the door and knock; if anyone hears My voice and opens the door, I will come in to him and dine with him, and he with Me.\f + \fr 3:20 \ft “Stand”: perfect (ἕστηκα), “I have taken My stand and remain”. “I will come in”: the Greek has “and I will come in” (καί introducing the consequence, in the Hebrew manner). “Dine”: Gr. δειπνέω; cf. δεῖπνον, the wedding supper (19:9).\f*
\p \v 21 The one who conquers, I will grant him to sit with Me on My throne, as I also conquered and sat down with My Father on His throne.\f + \fr 3:21 \ft “Grant”: Gr. δώσω, lit. “I will give”. Cf. 2:26–27; 22:1.\f* \v 22 Whoever has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches.
\c 4
\s1 The throne in heaven
\p \v 1 After these things I saw, and behold, an open door in heaven, and the first voice that I heard, like a trumpet speaking with me, said: Come up here, and I will show you the things that must happen after these things.\f + \fr 4:1 \ft “After these things”: Gr. μετὰ ταῦτα, as in 1:19; “the things that must happen” takes up 1:1. The SBLGNT punctuates the second “after these things” as the start of 4:2 (“After these things, immediately I was in the Spirit”); the punctuation followed here is that of the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus (without their “and” at the start of 4:2). “Like a trumpet”: cf. 1:10; Ex 19:16–20.\f*
\p \v 2 Immediately I was in the Spirit; and behold, a throne was set in heaven, and on the throne One seated;\f + \fr 4:2 \ft “I was”: Gr. ἐγενόμην, as in 1:10. “One seated”: cf. 1 Kgs 22:19; Isa 6:1; Ezek 1:26; Dan 7:9.\f* \v 3 and He who was seated was like a jasper stone and a sardius in appearance; and around the throne there was a rainbow like an emerald in appearance.\f + \fr 4:3 \ft “Jasper… sardius”: Gr. ἴασπις and σάρδιον, stones of uncertain identification (cf. 21:11, 19–20). “Rainbow”: cf. Gen 9:13; Ezek 1:28. Variant: the Byzantine text omits “and He who was seated”.\f* \v 4 Around the throne were twenty-four thrones, and on the thrones twenty-four elders seated, clothed in white garments, and on their heads golden crowns.\f + \fr 4:4 \ft “Elders”: Gr. πρεσβύτεροι. “Clothed”: Gr. περιβάλλω, lit. “to throw around”, as in 3:5. “Crowns”: Gr. στέφανος (cf. 2:10).\f* \v 5 From the throne come flashes of lightning, and voices, and thunders; and seven torches of fire burn before the throne, which are the Seven Spirits of God;\f + \fr 4:5 \ft “Voices”: Gr. φωναί, also “rumblings” (cf. Ex 19:16, Heb. קֹלֹת‎; Rev 8:5; 11:19; 16:18). “Torches”: Gr. λαμπάδες (cf. 8:10; Ezek 1:13; cf. the seven lamps of Zech 4:2). “Seven Spirits”: cf. 1:4.\f* \v 6 and before the throne, something like a sea of glass, like crystal. In the midst of the throne and around the throne were four living creatures, full of eyes in front and behind.\f + \fr 4:6 \ft “Sea of glass”: cf. 15:2; Ezek 1:22. “Living creatures”: Gr. ζῷα, lit. “living beings”; cf. Ezek 1:5, Heb. חַיּוֹת‎.\f* \v 7 The first living creature was like a lion; and the second living creature like a young bull; and the third living creature had a face like that of a man; and the fourth living creature was like a flying eagle.\f + \fr 4:7 \ft Cf. Ezek 1:10. “Young bull”: Gr. μόσχος.\f*
\p \v 8 The four living creatures, each of them with six wings, are full of eyes all around and within; and they have no rest, day and night, saying:
\q1 Holy, holy, holy,
\q1 the Lord God, the Almighty,
\q1 He Who Was, and Who Is, and Who Is Coming.\f + \fr 4:8 \ft Cf. Isa 6:2–3. “He Who Was, and Who Is, and Who Is Coming”: the formula of 1:4, 8, here with “Was” first. “They have no rest, day and night”: the same expression as in 14:11.\f*
\p \v 9 Whenever the living creatures give glory, and honor, and thanksgiving to Him who is seated on the throne, to Him who lives forever and ever,\f + \fr 4:9 \ft “Whenever… give”: Gr. ὅταν with the future, a repeated action. “Him who lives forever and ever”: cf. 1:18; 10:6; 15:7; Dan 4:34 (Aram. 4:31); 12:7.\f* \v 10 the twenty-four elders will fall down before Him who is seated on the throne, and will worship Him who lives forever and ever, and will cast their crowns before the throne, saying:\f + \fr 4:10 \ft “Will fall down”: Gr. πίπτω, lit. “fall”; before God, prostration in worship (cf. 1:17). “Will worship”: Gr. προσκυνέω, “worship” because the object is God (contrast 3:9, “bow down”). “Will cast”: Gr. βάλλω.\f*
\q1 \v 11 Worthy are You, our Lord and God,
\q1 to receive the glory, and the honor, and the power,
\q1 for You created all things,
\q1 and because of Your will they existed and were created.\f + \fr 4:11 \ft “Created”: Gr. κτίζω (cf. “creation”, 3:14). “Because of Your will”: Gr. διὰ τὸ θέλημά σου. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “Worthy are You, O Lord” (without “and our God”) and “they are and were created”; the Byzantine text adds “the Holy One” after “our Lord and God”.\f*
\c 5
\s1 The sealed book
\p \v 1 And I saw in the right hand of Him who is seated on the throne a book written inside and on the back, sealed with seven seals.\f + \fr 5:1 \ft “In the right hand”: Gr. ἐπὶ τὴν δεξιάν, lit. “on the right hand”. “Book”: Gr. βιβλίον, a scroll written on both sides and sealed along its edge, so that each seal opened in turn; “book” is kept throughout for the one Greek word (cf. 1:11; 6:14; 22:7). “On the back”: Gr. ὄπισθεν, lit. “behind”; cf. Ezek 2:9–10. “Sealed with seven seals”: cf. Isa 29:11; Dan 12:4. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “outside” (ἔξωθεν).\f*
\p \v 2 And I saw a mighty angel proclaiming with a loud voice: Who is worthy to open the book and to break its seals?\f + \fr 5:2 \ft “Mighty”: Gr. ἰσχυρός (cf. ἰσχύς, “strength”, 5:12). “Break”: Gr. λῦσαι, lit. “loose”, the same verb as in 1:5 (“freed”).\f* \v 3 But no one in heaven, or on the earth, or under the earth was able to open the book or to look at it. \v 4 I was weeping greatly, because no one was found worthy to open the book or to look at it.\f + \fr 5:4 \ft Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “to open and to read the book”.\f* \v 5 Then one of the elders says to me: Do not weep; behold, the Lion of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has conquered, to open the book and its seven seals.\f + \fr 5:5 \ft “The Lion of the tribe of Judah”: cf. Gen 49:9–10. “The Root of David”: cf. Isa 11:1, 10; Rev 22:16. “Has conquered”: Gr. νικάω (cf. 3:21). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “who opens the book”; the Textus Receptus, “to open the book and to loose its seven seals”.\f*
\s1 Worthy is the Lamb
\p \v 6 And I saw, in the midst of the throne and of the four living creatures, and in the midst of the elders, a Lamb standing, as though slain, having seven horns and seven eyes, which are the Seven Spirits of God, sent out into all the earth.\f + \fr 5:6 \ft “Lamb”: Gr. ἀρνίον, a title of Christ in the book (except 13:11). “Slain”: Gr. σφάζω, to slaughter in sacrifice (cf. Isa 53:7, Septuagint σφαγή; Jer 11:19). “Horns”: a symbol of power (cf. Deut 33:17; Ps 89:17). “Seven eyes… sent out into all the earth”: cf. Zech 4:10.\f* \v 7 He came and took it from the right hand of Him who is seated on the throne.\f + \fr 5:7 \ft “Took it”: a dramatic perfect (εἴληφεν), rendered as a simple past; the object, the book, is implied. The Textus Receptus makes it explicit (“took the book”).\f* \v 8 When He had taken the book, the four living creatures and the twenty-four elders fell down before the Lamb, each holding a harp and golden bowls full of incense, which are the prayers of the saints.\f + \fr 5:8 \ft “Fell down”: Gr. πίπτω (cf. 4:10). “Harp”: Gr. κιθάρα, a stringed instrument like the lyre (cf. 14:2; 15:2). “Bowls”: Gr. φιάλαι, the same word as the bowls of wrath (15:7; 16:1). “Prayers of the saints”: cf. Ps 141:2; Rev 8:3–4.\f* \v 9 They sing a new song, saying:
\q1 Worthy are You to take the book
\q1 and to open its seals,
\q1 for You were slain,
\q1 and by Your blood You purchased for God
\q1 those from every tribe, and tongue, and people, and nation;\f + \fr 5:9 \ft “New song”: cf. Ps 96:1; Isa 42:10; Rev 14:3. “Purchased”: Gr. ἀγοράζω (cf. 14:3–4). “Those from”: the Greek says only “from” (ἐκ), with no expressed object. Variant: Codex Sinaiticus, the Byzantine text, most manuscripts and the Textus Receptus read “purchased us” (ἡμᾶς); the SBLGNT follows Codex Alexandrinus, which omits it. “Tribe, and tongue, and people, and nation”: a formula repeated in the book (cf. 7:9; 14:6).\f*
\q1 \v 10 and You made them, for our God,
\q1 a kingdom and priests;
\q1 and they will reign on the earth.\f + \fr 5:10 \ft Cf. 1:6; Ex 19:6. “They will reign”: follows the reading βασιλεύσουσιν, of Codex Sinaiticus and other manuscripts (also in the Robinson-Pierpont Byzantine text and in Nestle 1904); the SBLGNT follows Codex Alexandrinus, which reads βασιλεύουσιν, “they reign”. Cf. 20:4, 6; 22:5. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “kings and priests”; the Textus Receptus, “made us kings and priests… and we will reign”.\f*
\p \v 11 And I saw, and I heard the voice of many angels around the throne and the living creatures and the elders; and their number was myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands,\f + \fr 5:11 \ft “Myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands”: cf. Dan 7:10.\f* \v 12 saying with a loud voice:
\q1 Worthy is the slain Lamb
\q1 to receive the power, and riches, and wisdom,
\q1 and strength, and honor, and glory, and blessing.\f + \fr 5:12 \ft “Slain Lamb”: a perfect participle (ἐσφαγμένον); the Lamb remains the one who was slain. Seven attributes, governed by a single article in the SBLGNT. “Blessing”: Gr. εὐλογία (cf. 5:13; 7:12).\f*
\p \v 13 And every creature that is in heaven, and on the earth, and under the earth, and on the sea, and all things that are in them, I heard saying:
\q1 To Him who is seated on the throne and to the Lamb
\q1 be the blessing, and the honor, and the glory, and the dominion,
\q1 forever and ever.\f + \fr 5:13 \ft “Creature”: Gr. κτίσμα (cf. κτίζω, 4:11). “Dominion”: Gr. κράτος (cf. 1:6).\f*
\p \v 14 The four living creatures were saying: Amen. And the elders fell down and worshiped.\f + \fr 5:14 \ft Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “the twenty-four elders… and worshiped Him who lives forever and ever”.\f*
\c 6
\s1 The first four seals
\p \v 1 And I saw when the Lamb opened one of the seven seals, and I heard one of the four living creatures saying, as with a voice of thunder: Come!\f + \fr 6:1 \ft “Come”: Gr. ἔρχου. Variant: the Textus Receptus adds “and see” (καὶ βλέπε) in 6:1, 3, 5 and 7; Codex Sinaiticus and the Byzantine text have a similar addition (καὶ ἴδε); in the Byzantine text, in 6:1, 5 and 7.\f* \v 2 And I saw, and behold, a white horse, and He who sat on it had a bow; a crown was given to Him, and He went out conquering and to conquer.\f + \fr 6:2 \ft “He who sat”: the capital identifies the rider with the rider of the white horse in 19:11–16; in the book, “white”, “crown” and “conquer” are linked to Christ and His people (cf. 3:21; 5:5; 14:14). Another reading sees here a conquering power. “Bow”: Gr. τόξον. “Crown”: Gr. στέφανος, the victor’s crown (cf. 2:10; 3:11). “Conquering and to conquer”: Gr. νικῶν καὶ ἵνα νικήσῃ (cf. νικάω, 5:5). Horses of different colors: cf. Zech 1:8; 6:1–8.\f*
\p \v 3 And when He opened the second seal, I heard the second living creature saying: Come! \v 4 Another horse came out, a red one. It was given to him who sat on it to take peace from the earth, and that people should slay one another; and a great sword was given to him.\f + \fr 6:4 \ft “Red”: Gr. πυρρός, lit. “fire-colored”. “Slay”: Gr. σφάζω, the same verb rendered “slain” in 5:6, 9, 12 and 6:9. “Sword”: Gr. μάχαιρα.\f*
\p \v 5 And when He opened the third seal, I heard the third living creature saying: Come! And I saw, and behold, a black horse, and he who sat on it had a balance in his hand.\f + \fr 6:5 \ft “Balance”: Gr. ζυγός, lit. “yoke”, the beam of the scales.\f* \v 6 And I heard something like a voice in the midst of the four living creatures, saying: A measure of wheat for a denarius, and three measures of barley for a denarius; and do not harm the oil and the wine.\f + \fr 6:6 \ft “Measure”: Gr. χοῖνιξ, a little more than a liter (about a quart). “Denarius”: a Roman silver coin (cf. Matt 20:2). “Do not harm”: Gr. ἀδικέω (cf. 2:11; 7:2–3; 9:4). Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “a voice”, without “something like”.\f*
\p \v 7 And when He opened the fourth seal, I heard the voice of the fourth living creature saying: Come! \v 8 And I saw, and behold, a pale horse, and he who sat on it had the name Death, and the grave was following him. Authority was given to them over a fourth of the earth, to kill with sword, and with famine, and with pestilence, and by the wild beasts of the earth.\f + \fr 6:8 \ft “Pale”: Gr. χλωρός, lit. “pale green” (cf. 8:7; 9:4, “green”). “The grave”: Gr. ᾅδης (cf. 1:18). “Sword”: here Gr. ῥομφαία (cf. 1:16). “Pestilence”: Gr. θάνατος, lit. “death”; the Septuagint uses the same word for “pestilence” in Ezek 14:21. Sword, famine, pestilence and wild beasts: cf. Ezek 14:21; Lev 26:22–26. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “was given to him” (singular); the Textus Receptus, like the SBLGNT, has the plural.\f*
\s1 The fifth seal
\p \v 9 And when He opened the fifth seal, I saw under the altar the souls of those who had been slain because of the Word of God and because of the testimony that they held.\f + \fr 6:9 \ft “Altar”: Gr. θυσιαστήριον. “Souls”: Gr. ψυχαί, also “lives” (cf. Lev 4:7; 17:11). “Slain”: Gr. σφάζω (cf. 5:6). “Word of God… testimony”: cf. 1:2, 9. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “the testimony of the Lamb”.\f* \v 10 They cried out with a loud voice, saying: How long, O Sovereign, the Holy and True One, do You not judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?\f + \fr 6:10 \ft “Sovereign”: Gr. δεσπότης, “master, owner” (cf. Luke 2:29; Acts 4:24). “The Holy and True One”: cf. 3:7. “How long”: cf. Ps 79:5, 10; Zech 1:12. “Avenge… on”: Gr. ἐκδικέω… ἐκ, lit. “avenge… from” (cf. 19:2; Deut 32:43). “Those who dwell on the earth”: cf. 3:10.\f* \v 11 Then a white robe was given to each of them, and they were told to rest a little while longer, until the number of their fellow servants and of their brothers, who were about to be killed as they also had been, was completed.\f + \fr 6:11 \ft “Robe”: Gr. στολή, a long robe (cf. 7:9, 13–14; 22:14). “The number”: the Greek says only “until both their fellow servants and their brothers… were completed”. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “until they complete [their course]” (πληρώσωσιν). “Fellow servants”: Gr. σύνδουλοι (cf. 19:10; 22:9). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “white robes were given to each”.\f*
\s1 The sixth seal
\p \v 12 And I saw when He opened the sixth seal, and there was a great earthquake, and the sun became black as sackcloth of hair, and the whole moon became like blood,\f + \fr 6:12 \ft “Earthquake”: Gr. σεισμός (cf. 8:5; 11:13, 19; 16:18). “Sackcloth of hair”: Gr. σάκκος τρίχινος, a dark cloth of hair, usually goat’s hair. Sun, moon and stars: cf. Joel 2:10, 31 (Heb. 3:4); Isa 13:10; Matt 24:29. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “and behold, there was” and omits “whole”.\f* \v 13 and the stars of heaven fell to the earth, as a fig tree casts its unripe figs when it is shaken by a great wind,\f + \fr 6:13 \ft “Casts”: Gr. βάλλω. “Unripe figs”: Gr. ὄλυνθοι, figs that fall before ripening. Cf. Isa 34:4.\f* \v 14 and heaven withdrew like a scroll being rolled up, and every mountain and island were moved from their places.\f + \fr 6:14 \ft “Scroll”: Gr. βιβλίον, “book”, here the scroll that is rolled up (cf. 5:1; Isa 34:4). “Were moved”: plural in the Greek.\f*
\p \v 15 The kings of the earth, and the great ones, and the commanders, and the rich, and the mighty, and every slave and free man hid themselves in the caves and among the rocks of the mountains;\f + \fr 6:15 \ft “The great ones”: Gr. μεγιστᾶνες, the nobles. “Commanders”: Gr. χιλίαρχοι, lit. “commanders of a thousand”. “Slave”: Gr. δοῦλος, rendered “servant” in 1:1; here in contrast with “free” (cf. 13:16; 19:18). Cf. Isa 2:10, 19, 21.\f* \v 16 and they say to the mountains and to the rocks: Fall on us and hide us from the face of Him who is seated on the throne and from the wrath of the Lamb,\f + \fr 6:16 \ft Cf. Hos 10:8; Luke 23:30. “Face”: Gr. πρόσωπον.\f* \v 17 for the great day of Their wrath has come, and who is able to stand?\f + \fr 6:17 \ft “Their”: of Him who is seated on the throne and of the Lamb (6:16). Variant: Codex Alexandrinus, the Robinson-Pierpont Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “of His wrath”; the SBLGNT is followed, with Codex Sinaiticus and Ephraemi. “Stand”: Gr. σταθῆναι (cf. 5:6, the Lamb “standing”; Nah 1:6; Mal 3:2). “The great day”: cf. Joel 2:11; Zeph 1:14.\f*
\c 7
\s1 The sealed of Israel
\p \v 1 After this I saw four angels standing on the four corners of the earth, holding the four winds of the earth, so that no wind would blow on the earth, or on the sea, or on any tree.\f + \fr 7:1 \ft “After this”: Gr. μετὰ τοῦτο (singular); in 7:9, μετὰ ταῦτα, “after these things” (cf. 4:1). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “And after this”; the Textus Receptus, “And after these things”. “Holding”: Gr. κρατέω (cf. 2:1). “Four corners… four winds”: cf. Isa 11:12; Jer 49:36; Dan 7:2; Zech 6:5.\f* \v 2 And I saw another angel ascending from the rising of the sun, having the seal of the living God; and he cried out with a loud voice to the four angels to whom it was given to harm the earth and the sea,\f + \fr 7:2 \ft “From the rising of the sun”: that is, from the east. “The seal of the living God”: Gr. σφραγῖδα θεοῦ ζῶντος, lit. “a seal of the living God”. “Harm”: Gr. ἀδικέω (cf. 6:6).\f* \v 3 saying: Do not harm the earth, or the sea, or the trees, until we have sealed the servants of our God on their foreheads.\f + \fr 7:3 \ft “Sealed”: Gr. σφραγίζω (cf. 10:4; 22:10). “Foreheads”: cf. 9:4; 14:1; 22:4; Ezek 9:4; Ex 28:36–38.\f*
\p \v 4 And I heard the number of those who were sealed: one hundred and forty-four thousand sealed, from every tribe of the sons of Israel: \v 5 from the tribe of Judah, twelve thousand sealed; from the tribe of Reuben, twelve thousand; from the tribe of Gad, twelve thousand;\f + \fr 7:5 \ft The list begins with Judah (cf. 5:5), includes Levi and Joseph, and does not include Dan or Ephraim (cf. Gen 49; Ezek 48). Variant: the Textus Receptus repeats “sealed” after each tribe.\f* \v 6 from the tribe of Asher, twelve thousand; from the tribe of Naphtali, twelve thousand; from the tribe of Manasseh, twelve thousand; \v 7 from the tribe of Simeon, twelve thousand; from the tribe of Levi, twelve thousand; from the tribe of Issachar, twelve thousand; \v 8 from the tribe of Zebulun, twelve thousand; from the tribe of Joseph, twelve thousand; from the tribe of Benjamin, twelve thousand sealed.
\s1 The great multitude
\p \v 9 After these things I saw, and behold, a great multitude that no one could count, from every nation, and tribes, and peoples, and tongues, standing before the throne and before the Lamb, clothed in white robes, and with palm branches in their hands;\f + \fr 7:9 \ft “From every nation, and tribes, and peoples, and tongues”: the order and number of the Greek, with a single preposition (cf. 5:9). “Clothed”: Gr. περιβάλλω (cf. 3:5). “Robes”: Gr. στολαί (cf. 6:11). “Palm branches”: Gr. φοίνικες (cf. Lev 23:40; John 12:13).\f* \v 10 and they cry out with a loud voice, saying:
\q1 Salvation to our God, who is seated on the throne,
\q1 and to the Lamb.\f + \fr 7:10 \ft “Salvation to our God”: the Greek has no verb (cf. 5:13; 19:1). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “crying out”.\f*
\p \v 11 All the angels were standing around the throne and around the elders and the four living creatures, and they fell down on their faces before the throne and worshiped God,\f + \fr 7:11 \ft “Were standing”: Gr. ἵστημι (cf. 5:6; 6:17). “Fell down”: Gr. πίπτω (cf. 4:10; 5:14).\f* \v 12 saying:
\q1 Amen. The blessing, and the glory, and the wisdom,
\q1 and the thanksgiving, and the honor, and the power, and the strength
\q1 be to our God forever and ever. Amen.\f + \fr 7:12 \ft Seven attributes, each with the article (in 5:12, a single article for all seven). “Thanksgiving”: Gr. εὐχαριστία (cf. 4:9).\f*
\p \v 13 Then one of the elders spoke up, saying to me: These who are clothed in the white robes, who are they, and where did they come from?\f + \fr 7:13 \ft “Spoke up”: Gr. ἀπεκρίθη, lit. “answered”, with no previous question.\f* \v 14 I said to him: My lord, you know. And he said to me:
\q1 These are the ones who came out of the great tribulation,
\q1 and they washed their robes
\q1 and made them white in the blood of the Lamb.\f + \fr 7:14 \ft “Said”: Gr. εἴρηκα, perfect. “My lord”: addressed to the elder; the Textus Receptus omits “my”. “Came out”: Gr. οἱ ἐρχόμενοι, present participle, lit. “those who are coming”; rendered in the past, in harmony with “washed… made white”. “The great tribulation”: Gr. θλῖψις (cf. 1:9; 2:22; Dan 12:1; Matt 24:21). “Washed… made white”: cf. 22:14; Gen 49:11; Isa 1:18; Dan 11:35; 12:10 (Heb.).\f*
\q1 \v 15 Therefore they are before the throne of God
\q1 and serve Him day and night in His sanctuary;
\q1 and He who is seated on the throne
\q1 will spread His tabernacle over them.\f + \fr 7:15 \ft “Serve”: Gr. λατρεύω, the service of worship. “Sanctuary”: Gr. ναός (cf. 3:12; 11:19). “Will spread His tabernacle”: Gr. σκηνόω, “to dwell in a tent” (cf. 13:6; 21:3; John 1:14; Ezek 37:27).\f*
\q1 \v 16 They will hunger no more, nor thirst any more,
\q1 nor will the sun by any means fall on them,
\q1 nor any scorching heat,\f + \fr 7:16 \ft Cf. Isa 49:10. “By any means”: Gr. οὐδὲ μή.\f*
\q1 \v 17 for the Lamb who is in the midst of the throne will shepherd them
\q1 and will guide them to springs of the waters of life;
\q1 and God will wipe away every tear from their eyes.\f + \fr 7:17 \ft “Will shepherd”: Gr. ποιμαίνω (cf. 2:27; Ps 23:1–2; Ezek 34:23). “To springs of the waters of life”: lit. “to springs of waters of life” (cf. Isa 49:10). Variants: the Byzantine text reads “shepherds… guides” (present); the Textus Receptus reads “to living springs of waters”. “Wipe away”: Gr. ἐξαλείφω (cf. 3:5; 21:4; Isa 25:8).\f*
\c 8
\s1 The seventh seal
\p \v 1 And when He opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven for about half an hour.\f + \fr 8:1 \ft Cf. Hab 2:20; Zeph 1:7; Zech 2:13 (Heb. 2:17).\f*
\p \v 2 And I saw the seven angels who stand before God, and seven trumpets were given to them.\f + \fr 8:2 \ft “Stand”: Gr. ἑστήκασιν, perfect. “Trumpets”: Gr. σάλπιγγες (cf. 1:10; 4:1; Num 10:9; Josh 6:4; Joel 2:1).\f* \v 3 Then another angel came and stood at the altar, having a golden censer. Much incense was given to him, so that he might add it to the prayers of all the saints on the golden altar that is before the throne.\f + \fr 8:3 \ft “At the altar”: Gr. ἐπὶ τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου, lit. “upon the altar”. “Censer”: Gr. λιβανωτός. “That he might add it to the prayers”: lit. “that he might give it to the prayers”. “Golden altar… before the throne”: cf. Ex 30:1–10; 40:5; Lev 16:12–13.\f* \v 4 From the hand of the angel, the smoke of the incense went up before God with the prayers of the saints.\f + \fr 8:4 \ft “With the prayers”: Gr. ταῖς προσευχαῖς, the same dative as in 8:3, rendered there “to the prayers” (cf. 5:8; Ps 141:2).\f* \v 5 Then the angel took the censer, filled it with fire from the altar, and cast it onto the earth; and there were thunders, and voices, and flashes of lightning, and an earthquake.\f + \fr 8:5 \ft “Took”: Gr. εἴληφεν, perfect (cf. 5:7). Cf. Lev 16:12; Ezek 10:2. “Thunders, and voices, and flashes of lightning”: cf. 4:5; 11:19; 16:18; Ex 19:16.\f*
\s1 The first four trumpets
\p \v 6 The seven angels who had the seven trumpets prepared themselves to blow their trumpets.\f + \fr 8:6 \ft “Blow their trumpets”: Gr. σαλπίζω, “to sound the trumpet”.\f* \v 7 And the first blew his trumpet: there came hail and fire mixed with blood, and they were cast upon the earth. And a third of the earth was burned up, and a third of the trees were burned up, and all green grass was burned up.\f + \fr 8:7 \ft “Hail and fire”: cf. Ex 9:23–25. “They were cast”: Gr. ἐβλήθη, singular. “Green”: Gr. χλωρός, the same term rendered “pale” in 6:8. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “the first angel” and omits “and a third of the earth was burned up”.\f*
\p \v 8 And the second angel blew his trumpet: something like a great mountain burning with fire was cast into the sea, and a third of the sea became blood,\f + \fr 8:8 \ft “Something like a great mountain”: Gr. ὡς ὄρος μέγα. Cf. Jer 51:25. “Became blood”: cf. Ex 7:20–21. Variant: the Byzantine text omits “with fire”; the Textus Receptus has it.\f* \v 9 and a third of the creatures that were in the sea, those that had life, died, and a third of the ships were destroyed.\f + \fr 8:9 \ft “Creatures”: Gr. κτίσματα (cf. 5:13). “That had life”: Gr. τὰ ἔχοντα ψυχάς, lit. “that had souls” (cf. 6:9; 16:3).\f*
\p \v 10 And the third angel blew his trumpet: a great star fell from heaven, burning like a torch, and it fell on a third of the rivers and on the springs of the waters.\f + \fr 8:10 \ft “Torch”: Gr. λαμπάς (cf. 4:5).\f* \v 11 The star is called Wormwood. A third of the waters became wormwood, and many people died from the waters, because they were made bitter.\f + \fr 8:11 \ft “The star is called Wormwood”: lit. “the name of the star is called: the Wormwood”. “Wormwood”: Gr. ἄψινθος, a bitter-tasting plant. Cf. Jer 9:15 (Heb. 9:14); 23:15; Amos 5:7; Ex 15:23.\f*
\p \v 12 And the fourth angel blew his trumpet: a third of the sun was struck, and a third of the moon, and a third of the stars, so that a third of them would be darkened, and the day would not shine for a third of it, and likewise the night.\f + \fr 8:12 \ft “Was struck”: Gr. πλήσσω. Cf. Ex 10:21–23; Joel 2:10; Amos 8:9.\f* \v 13 And I saw, and I heard an angel flying in midheaven, saying with a loud voice: Woe, woe, woe to those who dwell on the earth, because of the remaining voices of the trumpet of the three angels who are about to blow!\f + \fr 8:13 \ft “Angel”: follows the Textus Receptus and some manuscripts (ἀγγέλου; cf. 14:6, another angel “flying in midheaven”); the SBLGNT, codices Sinaiticus and Alexandrinus, and the Byzantine text read “eagle” (ἀετοῦ). “Midheaven”: Gr. μεσουράνημα, the highest point of the sky (cf. 14:6; 19:17). “Woe”: Gr. οὐαί (cf. 9:12; 11:14; 12:12). “Remaining”: Gr. λοιπαί.\f*
\c 9
\s1 The fifth trumpet
\p \v 1 And the fifth angel blew his trumpet: I saw a star that had fallen from heaven to the earth, and the key to the shaft of the abyss was given to him.\f + \fr 9:1 \ft “That had fallen”: Gr. πεπτωκότα, perfect participle. “Shaft of the abyss”: Gr. φρέαρ τῆς ἀβύσσου (cf. 11:7; 17:8; 20:1–3).\f* \v 2 He opened the shaft of the abyss, and smoke went up from the shaft like the smoke of a great furnace, and the sun and the air were darkened by the smoke of the shaft.\f + \fr 9:2 \ft “Furnace”: cf. Gen 19:28; Ex 19:18. “Were darkened”: cf. Joel 2:10. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “a burning furnace”.\f*
\p \v 3 From the smoke came locusts upon the earth, and authority was given to them, as the scorpions of the earth have authority.\f + \fr 9:3 \ft “Locusts”: cf. Ex 10:12–15; Joel 1:4; 2:4–10.\f* \v 4 They were told not to harm the grass of the earth, or any green thing, or any tree, except the people who do not have the seal of God on the foreheads.\f + \fr 9:4 \ft “Green thing”: Gr. χλωρόν, lit. “green” (cf. 8:7). “Except”: Gr. εἰ μή. “The seal of God on the foreheads”: cf. 7:2–3; Ezek 9:4, 6. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “on their foreheads”; the Textus Receptus also reads “only the people”.\f* \v 5 And it was given to them not to kill them, but that they should be tormented for five months; and their torment was like the torment of a scorpion when it strikes a person.\f + \fr 9:5 \ft “Tormented”: Gr. βασανίζω (cf. 14:10; 20:10). “Five months”: cf. 9:10. “Strikes”: Gr. παίω, “to hit”.\f* \v 6 In those days people will seek death and will by no means find it; and they will long to die, and death flees from them.\f + \fr 9:6 \ft “By no means”: Gr. οὐ μή; the Textus Receptus reads “will not find”. “Flees”: present in the Greek; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “will flee”. Cf. Job 3:21; Jer 8:3.\f*
\p \v 7 The forms of the locusts were like horses prepared for war; and on their heads were something like crowns resembling gold, and their faces were like the faces of men,\f + \fr 9:7 \ft “Forms”: Gr. ὁμοιώματα, lit. “likenesses”. “Horses prepared for war”: cf. Joel 2:4–5; Job 39:19–25. “Something like crowns”: Gr. ὡς στέφανοι.\f* \v 8 and they had hair like the hair of women, and their teeth were like those of lions,\f + \fr 9:8 \ft “Teeth… of lions”: cf. Joel 1:6.\f* \v 9 and they had breastplates like breastplates of iron, and the sound of their wings was like the sound of chariots of many horses running to war.\f + \fr 9:9 \ft “Sound”: Gr. φωνή, “voice”. Cf. Joel 2:5.\f* \v 10 They have tails like scorpions, and stings; and in their tails is their authority to harm people for five months.\f + \fr 9:10 \ft “Stings”: Gr. κέντρα. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “and there were stings in their tails”; the Byzantine text, “and they have authority to harm”.\f* \v 11 They have over them as king the angel of the abyss; his name in Hebrew is Abaddon, and in Greek he has the name Apollyon.\f + \fr 9:11 \ft “Abaddon”: Heb. אֲבַדּוֹן‎, “destruction” (cf. Job 26:6; 28:22; Ps 88:11 (Heb. 88:12); Prov 15:11). “Apollyon”: Gr. Ἀπολλύων, “destroyer”.\f*
\p \v 12 The first woe has passed; behold, two woes are still coming after these things.\f + \fr 9:12 \ft “Woe”: Gr. οὐαί (cf. 8:13; 11:14).\f*
\s1 The sixth trumpet
\p \v 13 And the sixth angel blew his trumpet: I heard a voice from the four horns of the golden altar that is before God,\f + \fr 9:13 \ft “A voice”: Gr. φωνὴν μίαν, lit. “a single voice”. “Horns of the golden altar”: cf. Ex 30:1–3; Rev 8:3. “Four”: Gr. τεσσάρων, following the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus; the SBLGNT follows Papyrus 47 and Codex Alexandrinus, which omit “four”.\f* \v 14 saying to the sixth angel, who had the trumpet: Release the four angels who are bound at the great river Euphrates.\f + \fr 9:14 \ft “Release”: Gr. λῦσον. “Bound”: Gr. δεδεμένους, perfect participle. “The great river Euphrates”: cf. 16:12; Gen 15:18; Isa 8:7.\f* \v 15 Then the four angels were released, who had been prepared for the hour, and day, and month, and year, to kill a third of mankind.\f + \fr 9:15 \ft “For the hour, and day, and month, and year”: a single article for the four terms, which may indicate an appointed moment. Alternative reading: a period formed by adding the four terms together (cf. Num 14:34; Ezek 4:6, a day for a year). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “for the hour and for the day”.\f* \v 16 The number of the armies of the cavalry was two hundred million; I heard their number.\f + \fr 9:16 \ft “Two hundred million”: Gr. δισμυριάδες μυριάδων, lit. “two myriads of myriads”, that is, twice ten thousand times ten thousand (cf. 5:11; Dan 7:10). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “myriads of myriads”.\f*
\p \v 17 Thus I saw the horses in the vision, and those who sat on them, having breastplates the color of fire, and of jacinth, and of sulfur; and the heads of the horses were like the heads of lions, and out of their mouths come fire, and smoke, and sulfur.\f + \fr 9:17 \ft “The color of fire… of jacinth… of sulfur”: Gr. πυρίνους, ὑακινθίνους, θειώδεις, lit. “of fire, of jacinth, of sulfur”; jacinth is a dark-blue or purple stone. “Fire, and smoke, and sulfur”: cf. Gen 19:24, 28; Rev 14:10; 19:20; 21:8.\f* \v 18 By these three plagues a third of mankind was killed: by the fire, and by the smoke, and by the sulfur that came out of their mouths.\f + \fr 9:18 \ft “Plagues”: Gr. πληγαί (cf. 15:1; 16:9). Variant: the Textus Receptus omits “plagues”.\f* \v 19 For the authority of the horses is in their mouth and in their tails; for their tails are like serpents, having heads, and with them they do harm.\f + \fr 9:19 \ft Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “their authority” in place of “the authority of the horses”.\f*
\p \v 20 The rest of mankind, who were not killed by these plagues, even so did not repent of the works of their hands, so as not to worship the demons and the idols of gold, and of silver, and of bronze, and of stone, and of wood, which can neither see, nor hear, nor walk;\f + \fr 9:20 \ft “Even so did not”: Gr. οὐδέ; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “did not”. “The rest”: Gr. λοιποί (cf. 19:21; 20:5). “So as not to worship”: Gr. ἵνα μή. “Idols… which can neither see”: cf. Deut 4:28; Ps 115:4–7; 135:15–17; Dan 5:23.\f* \v 21 and they did not repent of their murders, nor of their sorceries, nor of their prostitution, nor of their thefts.\f + \fr 9:21 \ft “Sorceries”: Gr. φάρμακα, lit. “drugs, potions”, here in the sense of spells; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read φαρμακειῶν, “sorceries”. “Prostitution”: Gr. πορνεία (cf. 2:21).\f*
\c 10
\s1 The angel and the little book
\p \v 1 And I saw another mighty Angel coming down from heaven, clothed in a cloud, and the rainbow on His head, and His face like the sun, and His feet like pillars of fire,\f + \fr 10:1 \ft “Angel”: the capital identifies this Angel with Christ; the rainbow, the face like the sun and the feet like pillars of fire recall the divine figures of 1:15–16 and 4:3 and the man clothed in linen of Dan 10:5–6; 12:7. Another reading sees here a mighty angel (cf. 5:2; 18:21). “Clothed in a cloud”: Gr. περιβάλλω (cf. 3:5). “The rainbow”: Gr. ἡ ἶρις (cf. 4:3). “His face like the sun”: cf. 1:16. “Pillars of fire”: cf. Ex 13:21. Variant: the Byzantine text omits “another”.\f* \v 2 and He had in His hand an open little book. He set His right foot on the sea, and His left on the earth,\f + \fr 10:2 \ft “Little book”: Gr. βιβλαρίδιον, a diminutive of βιβλίον, “book” (cf. 5:1; 10:8); the Byzantine text reads βιβλίον. “Open”: Gr. ἠνεῳγμένον, perfect participle.\f* \v 3 and He cried out with a loud voice, as a lion roars. When He cried out, the seven thunders uttered their voices.\f + \fr 10:3 \ft “As a lion roars”: Gr. μυκᾶται, lit. “bellows” (cf. Hos 11:10; Amos 3:8). “Uttered their voices”: lit. “spoke their own voices”. “The seven thunders”: cf. Ps 29:3–9.\f* \v 4 When the seven thunders had spoken, I was about to write, and I heard a voice from heaven, saying: Seal up what the seven thunders have spoken, and do not write it.\f + \fr 10:4 \ft “Seal up”: Gr. σφράγισον (cf. Dan 8:26; 12:4, 9; Rev 22:10).\f*
\p \v 5 Then the Angel whom I saw standing on the sea and on the earth raised His right hand to heaven,\f + \fr 10:5 \ft “Raised His right hand to heaven”: cf. Dan 12:7; Deut 32:40. Variant: the Textus Receptus omits “right”.\f* \v 6 and swore by Him who lives forever and ever, who created heaven and the things in it, and the earth and the things in it, and the sea and the things in it: There will be no more time;\f + \fr 10:6 \ft “Him who lives forever and ever”: cf. 4:9–10. “Who created heaven… and the sea”: cf. 14:7; Ex 20:11; Neh 9:6. “There will be no more time”: Gr. ὅτι χρόνος οὐκέτι ἔσται, lit. “that time will be no longer”; alternative reading: “there will be no more delay”.\f* \v 7 but in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he is about to blow his trumpet, the mystery of God will be completed, as He announced the good news to His servants the prophets.\f + \fr 10:7 \ft “Will be completed”: Gr. καὶ ἐτελέσθη, aorist, lit. “and was completed”, with a future sense (cf. 15:1); the Textus Receptus reads τελεσθῇ, “should be completed”. “Mystery”: Gr. μυστήριον (cf. 1:20; 17:5, 7). “Announced the good news”: Gr. εὐηγγέλισεν (cf. 14:6; Amos 3:7).\f*
\p \v 8 The voice that I had heard from heaven spoke with me again, saying: Go, take the open book in the hand of the Angel who is standing on the sea and on the earth.\f + \fr 10:8 \ft “Book”: here Gr. βιβλίον; the Textus Receptus reads βιβλαρίδιον, and the Byzantine text βιβλιδάριον, both “little book”.\f* \v 9 I went to the Angel, telling Him to give me the little book. And He says to me: Take it and devour it; it will make your stomach bitter, but in your mouth it will be sweet as honey.\f + \fr 10:9 \ft “Devour it”: Gr. κατάφαγε, an intensive form of “eat”. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “saying to him: Give me the little book”. “Stomach”: Gr. κοιλία. Cf. Ezek 2:8–3:3; Jer 15:16 (Heb.); Ps 119:103.\f* \v 10 I took the little book from the hand of the Angel and devoured it; in my mouth it was sweet as honey, but when I had eaten it, my stomach became bitter.\f + \fr 10:10 \ft “Became bitter”: Gr. ἐπικράνθη (cf. 8:11). Variant: the Byzantine text reads βιβλίον, “book”, for “little book”.\f* \v 11 And they say to me: You must prophesy again about many peoples, and nations, and tongues, and kings.\f + \fr 10:11 \ft “They say to me”: plural in the Greek; the Textus Receptus reads “he says to me”. “About”: Gr. ἐπί with the dative, also “before” (in the presence of) or “against”. “Many”: in the Greek it comes after “kings” (βασιλεῦσιν πολλοῖς), but it can apply to all the terms. “Peoples, and nations, and tongues, and kings”: cf. 5:9; 7:9; 17:15.\f*
\c 11
\s1 The measuring of the sanctuary
\p \v 1 A reed like a rod was given to me, and I was told: Rise and measure the sanctuary of God, and the altar, and those who worship in it.\f + \fr 11:1 \ft “Reed”: Gr. κάλαμος, “reed, cane”, used as a measuring rod; Ezekiel’s was six long cubits, about 3 meters (10 feet) (Ezek 40:3–5; cf. 21:15–16). “Like a rod”: Gr. ὅμοιος ῥάβδῳ; ῥάβδος is “rod, staff”, the same word as in 2:27; 12:5; 19:15 (“rod of iron”). “I was told”: Gr. λέγων, lit. “saying”, with no expressed subject; the Textus Receptus reads “and the angel stood, saying”. “Sanctuary”: Gr. ναός (cf. 3:12; 7:15). “Measure”: cf. Ezek 40–42; Zech 2:1–2 (Heb. 2:5–6).\f* \v 2 And the court that is outside the sanctuary, leave it out, and do not measure it, for it was given to the nations; and they will trample the holy city for forty-two months.\f + \fr 11:2 \ft “Leave it out”: Gr. ἔκβαλε ἔξωθεν, lit. “cast it outside”. “Trample”: cf. Luke 21:24; Dan 8:13. “Forty-two months”: cf. 11:3; 12:6, 14; 13:5; Dan 7:25; 12:7.\f*
\s1 The two witnesses
\p \v 3 And I will grant My two witnesses to prophesy for one thousand two hundred and sixty days, clothed in sackcloth.\f + \fr 11:3 \ft “I will grant… to prophesy”: lit. “I will give… and they will prophesy” (cf. 3:21). “Witnesses”: Gr. μάρτυσιν (cf. 1:5; 2:13). “One thousand two hundred and sixty days”: cf. 12:6. “Sackcloth”: Gr. σάκκους (cf. 6:12).\f* \v 4 These are the two olive trees and the two lampstands that stand before the Lord of the earth.\f + \fr 11:4 \ft “Olive trees… lampstands”: cf. Zech 4:2–3, 11–14; Rev 1:20. “Lord of the earth”: the Textus Receptus reads “God of the earth”.\f* \v 5 And if anyone wants to harm them, fire comes out of their mouth and devours their enemies; and if anyone would harm them, in this way he must be killed.\f + \fr 11:5 \ft “Fire… devours their enemies”: cf. 2 Kgs 1:10–12; Jer 5:14.\f* \v 6 These have authority to shut the sky, so that no rain may fall during the days of their prophecy; and they have authority over the waters to turn them into blood, and to strike the earth with every plague, as often as they wish.\f + \fr 11:6 \ft “Shut the sky… rain”: cf. 1 Kgs 17:1; Jas 5:17. “Waters… into blood”: cf. Ex 7:17–20. “Plague”: Gr. πληγή (cf. 9:18).\f*
\p \v 7 And when they have completed their testimony, the beast that comes up out of the abyss will make war against them, and conquer them, and kill them.\f + \fr 11:7 \ft “Completed”: Gr. τελέσωσιν (cf. 10:7). “Beast”: Gr. θηρίον (cf. 13:1; 17:8; Dan 7:3, 7, 21). “Comes up out of the abyss”: cf. 9:1; 17:8.\f* \v 8 Their dead body will lie in the street of the great city that is spiritually called Sodom and Egypt, where also their Lord was crucified.\f + \fr 11:8 \ft “Dead body”: Gr. τὸ πτῶμα, singular, also in 11:9a; plural in 11:9b. Variants: the Textus Receptus reads “dead bodies” (also in 11:9a) and “our Lord”. “Will lie”: the Greek has no verb. “Spiritually”: Gr. πνευματικῶς. “Sodom and Egypt”: cf. Isa 1:9–10; Ezek 16:46–49.\f* \v 9 Some from the peoples, and tribes, and tongues, and nations look at their dead body for three and a half days, and do not permit their dead bodies to be placed in a tomb.\f + \fr 11:9 \ft “Some from”: Gr. ἐκ τῶν, lit. “from among” (cf. 5:9). “Peoples, and tribes, and tongues, and nations”: cf. 5:9; 7:9; 10:11. “Tomb”: Gr. μνῆμα.\f* \v 10 Those who dwell on the earth rejoice over them and are glad, and will send gifts to one another, because these two prophets tormented those who dwell on the earth.\f + \fr 11:10 \ft “Tormented”: Gr. βασανίζω (cf. 9:5). Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “will not permit” (11:9) and “will be glad”; the Textus Receptus also reads “will look” (11:9).\f*
\p \v 11 After the three and a half days, a breath of life from God entered them, and they stood on their feet, and great fear fell on those who were watching them.\f + \fr 11:11 \ft “Breath of life”: Gr. πνεῦμα ζωῆς, “spirit, breath of life” (cf. Gen 2:7, Septuagint πνοή; Ezek 37:5, 10); the Septuagint uses πνεῦμα ζωῆς in Gen 7:15 and Ezek 37:5. “Stood on their feet”: cf. Ezek 37:10.\f* \v 12 Then they heard a loud voice from heaven saying to them: Come up here. And they went up to heaven in the cloud, and their enemies watched them.\f + \fr 11:12 \ft “Come up here”: cf. 4:1. “In the cloud”: cf. 2 Kgs 2:11; Acts 1:9. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “And I heard”.\f* \v 13 In that hour there was a great earthquake, and a tenth of the city fell, and seven thousand names of men were killed in the earthquake; and the rest were terrified and gave glory to the God of heaven.\f + \fr 11:13 \ft “Seven thousand names of men”: Gr. ὀνόματα ἀνθρώπων, that is, persons (cf. 3:4). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “in that day”. “The rest”: Gr. λοιποί (cf. 9:20). “Gave glory”: cf. 14:7; 16:9.\f* \v 14 The second woe has passed; behold, the third woe is coming soon.\f + \fr 11:14 \ft “Soon”: Gr. ταχύ (cf. 2:16; 22:7). “Woe”: cf. 8:13; 9:12.\f*
\s1 The seventh trumpet
\p \v 15 And the seventh angel blew his trumpet: there were loud voices in heaven, saying:
\q1 The kingdom of the world has become
\q1 our Lord’s and His Christ’s,
\q1 and He will reign forever and ever.\f + \fr 11:15 \ft “Has become”: Gr. ἐγένετο. “His Christ”: Gr. τοῦ χριστοῦ αὐτοῦ, “His Anointed” (cf. Ps 2:2; Dan 2:44; 7:14, 27). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “the kingdoms of the world have become”.\f*
\p \v 16 Then the twenty-four elders who sit before God on their thrones fell down on their faces and worshiped God,\f + \fr 11:16 \ft “Before God”: the Byzantine text reads “before the throne of God”.\f* \v 17 saying:
\q1 We give You thanks, O Lord God, the Almighty,
\q1 He Who Is, and Who Was,
\q1 for You have taken Your great power
\q1 and have begun to reign.\f + \fr 11:17 \ft “He Who Is, and Who Was”: without “and Who Is Coming” (cf. 1:4, 8; 4:8); the Textus Receptus adds “and Who Is Coming”. “Have taken”: Gr. εἴληφας, perfect. “Have begun to reign”: Gr. ἐβασίλευσας, lit. “You reigned”.\f*
\q1 \v 18 And the nations were enraged,
\q1 and Your wrath has come,
\q1 and the time for the dead to be judged,
\q1 and for giving the reward to Your servants the prophets,
\q1 and to the saints, and to those who fear Your name,
\q1 the small and the great,
\q1 and for destroying those who destroy the earth.\f + \fr 11:18 \ft “The nations were enraged”: cf. Ps 2:1–5; 99:1 [LXX 98:1]. “The time for the dead to be judged”: Gr. ὁ καιρὸς τῶν νεκρῶν κριθῆναι; καιρός = “time” (cf. 1:3; Dan 7:10, 22). “Reward”: Gr. μισθός (cf. 22:12). “The small and the great”: cf. 13:16; 19:5; 20:12; Ps 115:13 [LXX 113:21]. “Destroying those who destroy”: Gr. διαφθεῖραι… διαφθείροντας, also “corrupt”.\f*
\p \v 19 The sanctuary of God that is in heaven was opened, and the ark of His covenant was seen in His sanctuary; and there were flashes of lightning, and voices, and thunders, and an earthquake, and great hail.\f + \fr 11:19 \ft “Sanctuary… in heaven”: Gr. ναός (cf. 15:5; Heb 8:1–5; 9:24). “Ark of His covenant”: Gr. κιβωτὸς τῆς διαθήκης (cf. Ex 25:10–22; Deut 10:1–5; Heb 9:4). “Flashes of lightning, and voices, and thunders”: cf. 4:5; 8:5; 16:18. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “the ark of the covenant of the Lord” and omits “and an earthquake”.\f*
\c 12
\s1 The woman and the dragon
\p \v 1 And a great sign appeared in heaven: a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars;\f + \fr 12:1 \ft “Sign”: Gr. σημεῖον (cf. 12:3; 15:1). “Clothed with the sun”: Gr. περιβάλλω (cf. 3:5). “Twelve stars”: cf. Gen 37:9. “Crown”: Gr. στέφανος (cf. 2:10).\f* \v 2 and she is with child; and she cries out, in labor pains and in torment to give birth.\f + \fr 12:2 \ft “Cries out”: present in the Greek, as in the Textus Receptus; the Byzantine text reads “cried out”. “Labor pains”: Gr. ὠδίνω (cf. Isa 26:17; 66:7–8; Mic 4:10). “In torment”: Gr. βασανίζω (cf. 9:5).\f*
\p \v 3 And another sign appeared in heaven, and behold, a great red dragon, having seven heads and ten horns, and on his heads seven diadems.\f + \fr 12:3 \ft “Red”: Gr. πυρρός, lit. “fire-colored” (cf. 6:4). “Seven heads and ten horns”: cf. 13:1; 17:3, 7; Dan 7:7, 20, 24. “Diadems”: Gr. διαδήματα (cf. 13:1; 19:12).\f* \v 4 His tail sweeps away a third of the stars of heaven, and cast them to the earth. The dragon was standing before the woman who was about to give birth, so that when she gave birth he might devour her Child.\f + \fr 12:4 \ft “Sweeps away”: present in the Greek. “A third of the stars”: cf. Dan 8:10. “Was standing”: Gr. ἕστηκεν, perfect. “Devour”: Gr. καταφάγῃ (cf. 10:9). “Child”: Gr. τέκνον; for the capital, cf. 12:5.\f* \v 5 She gave birth to a Son, a male Child, who is about to shepherd all the nations with a rod of iron; and her Child was caught up to God and to His throne.\f + \fr 12:5 \ft “A Son, a male Child”: Gr. υἱὸν ἄρσεν (cf. Isa 66:7); the capital follows the identification given in the verse itself, the rod of iron of Ps 2:9. “Is about to”: Gr. μέλλει (cf. 1:19). “Shepherd… with a rod of iron”: cf. 2:27; 19:15; Ps 2:9. “Caught up”: Gr. ἡρπάσθη, “seized, snatched away”.\f* \v 6 The woman fled into the wilderness, where she has a place prepared by God, so that there they may nourish her for one thousand two hundred and sixty days.\f + \fr 12:6 \ft “Wilderness”: Gr. ἔρημος (cf. 12:14; 17:3). “They may nourish”: Gr. τρέφωσιν, an impersonal plural. “One thousand two hundred and sixty days”: cf. 11:3; 12:14; 13:5.\f*
\s1 The war in heaven
\p \v 7 And there was war in heaven: Michael and His angels made war against the dragon. And the dragon and his angels made war,\f + \fr 12:7 \ft “Michael”: cf. Dan 10:13, 21; 12:1; Jude 9. “His”: the capital identifies Michael with Christ: the “great prince” who stands up over “the sons of your people” (Dan 12:1), the archangel (Jude 9) whose voice accompanies the Lord’s descent (1 Thess 4:16). Another reading sees here a created archangel, distinct from Christ. “Made war”: lit. “Michael and his angels to make war”, an infinitive in the Greek (τοῦ πολεμῆσαι); the Textus Receptus has the finite verb (ἐπολέμησαν).\f* \v 8 and he did not prevail, nor was a place found for them any longer in heaven.\f + \fr 12:8 \ft “He did not prevail”: singular in the Greek (the dragon), with Codex Alexandrinus and the Byzantine text; Codex Sinaiticus and the Textus Receptus read the plural, “they did not prevail”. The Byzantine text also reads “for him” in place of “for them”.\f* \v 9 And the great dragon was cast down, the ancient serpent, who is called the devil and Satan, who deceives the whole world; he was cast down to the earth, and his angels were cast down with him.\f + \fr 12:9 \ft “The ancient serpent”: cf. 20:2; Gen 3:1, 14. “devil”: Gr. διάβολος, “accuser, slanderer”. “Satan”: Heb. “adversary” (cf. Job 1:6; Zech 3:1). “Deceives”: Gr. πλανάω (cf. 2:20). “The whole world”: cf. 3:10.\f*
\p \v 10 And I heard a loud voice in heaven, saying:
\q1 Now have come the salvation, and the power,
\q1 and the kingdom of our God,
\q1 and the authority of His Christ,
\q1 for the accuser of our brothers was cast down,
\q1 who accuses them before our God day and night.\f + \fr 12:10 \ft “Now”: Gr. ἄρτι. “His Christ”: cf. 11:15. “Accuser”: Gr. κατήγωρ (cf. Job 1:9–11; Zech 3:1).\f*
\q1 \v 11 And they conquered him because of the blood of the Lamb
\q1 and because of the word of their testimony,
\q1 and they did not love their life even to death.\f + \fr 12:11 \ft “Because of”: Gr. διά with the accusative. “Testimony”: Gr. μαρτυρία (cf. 6:9). “Life”: Gr. ψυχή (cf. Mark 8:35; John 12:25).\f*
\q1 \v 12 Therefore rejoice, O heavens,
\q1 and you who have your tabernacle in them.
\q1 Woe to the earth and the sea,
\q1 for the devil has come down to you,
\q1 having great fury,
\q1 knowing that he has little time.\f + \fr 12:12 \ft “Rejoice”: Gr. εὐφραίνω (cf. 11:10). “Have your tabernacle”: Gr. σκηνοῦντες, “dwelling in a tent” (cf. 7:15; 13:6), in contrast with “those who dwell on the earth” (κατοικέω). “Woe”: Gr. οὐαί (cf. 8:13). “Fury”: Gr. θυμός (cf. 14:8, 10; 16:19). “Little time”: Gr. ὀλίγον καιρόν. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “Woe to those who dwell on the earth and the sea”.\f*
\s1 The dragon pursues the woman
\p \v 13 When the dragon saw that he had been cast down to the earth, he pursued the woman who had given birth to the male Child.\f + \fr 12:13 \ft “The male Child”: Gr. τὸν ἄρσενα (cf. 12:5).\f* \v 14 The two wings of the great eagle were given to the woman, so that she might fly into the wilderness, to her place, where she is nourished for a time, and times, and half a time, away from the face of the serpent.\f + \fr 12:14 \ft “Great eagle”: cf. Ex 19:4; Deut 32:11; Isa 40:31. “A time, and times, and half a time”: Gr. καιρὸν καὶ καιροὺς καὶ ἥμισυ καιροῦ (cf. 12:6; Dan 7:25; 12:7). “Away from the face”: Gr. ἀπὸ προσώπου. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “so that she may be nourished”.\f* \v 15 Then the serpent cast water like a river out of his mouth after the woman, so that she would be swept away by the river.\f + \fr 12:15 \ft “Swept away by the river”: Gr. ποταμοφόρητον. Cf. Ps 18:4 (Heb. 18:5); 124:4; Isa 43:2.\f* \v 16 But the earth helped the woman, and the earth opened its mouth and swallowed the river that the dragon had cast out of his mouth.\f + \fr 12:16 \ft “Swallowed”: cf. Num 16:30–32.\f* \v 17 Then the dragon was enraged with the woman, and went off to make war against the remnant of her offspring, those who keep the commandments of God and have the Testimony of Jesus. And he stood on the sand of the sea.\f + \fr 12:17 \ft “Remnant”: Gr. οἱ λοιποί (cf. 2:24). “Offspring”: Gr. σπέρμα, lit. “seed” (cf. Gen 3:15). “Keep the commandments of God”: cf. 14:12. “The Testimony of Jesus”: cf. 1:2, 9; 19:10. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “the Testimony of Jesus Christ”. “And he stood”: the dragon. In the critical editions of the Greek this sentence is v. 18; here the traditional verse count is kept and the sentence is joined to v. 17. The text follows the SBLGNT, with Papyrus 47 and codices Sinaiticus and Alexandrinus. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “And I stood” and place the sentence at the beginning of 13:1.\f*
\c 13
\s1 The beast that rises from the sea
\p \v 1 And I saw a beast coming up out of the sea, having ten horns and seven heads, and on its horns ten diadems, and on its heads names of blasphemy.\f + \fr 13:1 \ft “Coming up out of the sea”: cf. Dan 7:3. “Ten horns and seven heads”: cf. 12:3; 17:3, 7, 12; Dan 7:7, 24; the Textus Receptus reverses the order (“seven heads and ten horns”). “Names of blasphemy”: the Textus Receptus reads “name”. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus begin the verse with “And I stood on the sand of the sea” (cf. 12:17).\f* \v 2 The beast that I saw was like a leopard, and its feet were like those of a bear, and its mouth like the mouth of a lion. The dragon gave it his power, and his throne, and great authority.\f + \fr 13:2 \ft “Leopard… bear… lion”: cf. Dan 7:4–6. “Power… throne… authority”: Gr. δύναμις, θρόνος, ἐξουσία (cf. 2:13; 12:9).\f*
\p \v 3 And one of its heads was as though slain to death, and its mortal wound was healed. And the whole earth marveled, following the beast,\f + \fr 13:3 \ft “And one of its heads was”: lit. “and one of its heads”, with no verb; the Textus Receptus reads “And I saw one of its heads”. “As though slain to death”: Gr. ὡς ἐσφαγμένην εἰς θάνατον, the same participle used for the Lamb (5:6, “as though slain”, in the neuter). “Mortal wound”: Gr. ἡ πληγὴ τοῦ θανάτου, lit. “the wound of its death”; πληγή, also “plague” (cf. 9:18). “Following the beast”: lit. “after the beast”.\f* \v 4 and they worshiped the dragon, because he had given his authority to the beast; and they worshiped the beast, saying: Who is like the beast? And who is able to make war against it?\f + \fr 13:4 \ft “Who is like”: cf. Ex 15:11; Ps 35:10 [LXX 34:10]; 89:6–8 (Heb. 89:7–9) [LXX 88:7–9]; Mic 7:18.\f*
\p \v 5 A mouth was given to it, speaking great things and blasphemies, and authority was given to it to act for forty-two months.\f + \fr 13:5 \ft “Great things and blasphemies”: cf. Dan 7:8, 11, 20, 25. “To act”: Gr. ποιῆσαι, “to do, to act”; alternative reading: “to continue”. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “blasphemy” (singular) and “to make war”. “Forty-two months”: cf. 11:2; 12:6, 14.\f* \v 6 It opened its mouth in blasphemies against God, to blaspheme His name and His tabernacle, those who have their tabernacle in heaven.\f + \fr 13:6 \ft “Tabernacle… those who have their tabernacle in heaven”: Gr. σκηνή… σκηνοῦντας (cf. 7:15; 12:12; 21:3); the Textus Receptus reads “and those who”; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “blasphemy”, singular. Cf. Dan 8:10–11; 11:36.\f* \v 7 And it was granted to make war against the saints and to conquer them; and authority was given to it over every tribe, and people, and tongue, and nation.\f + \fr 13:7 \ft “Make war against the saints and to conquer them”: cf. 11:7; Dan 7:21, 25. “Every tribe, and people, and tongue, and nation”: cf. 5:9; 7:9; 14:6; the Textus Receptus omits “and people”.\f* \v 8 And all who dwell on the earth will worship it, everyone whose name is not written in the book of life of the slain Lamb from the foundation of the world.\f + \fr 13:8 \ft “Worship it”: Gr. αὐτόν, masculine. “Book of life”: cf. 3:5; 17:8; 20:12, 15; 21:27. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read ὧν, “all whose name” (the Textus Receptus, “whose names”). “From the foundation of the world”: alternative reading: it goes with “written”, “whose name is not written from the foundation of the world” (cf. 17:8).\f*
\q1 \v 9 If anyone has an ear, let him hear.\f + \fr 13:9 \ft Cf. 2:7, 11, 17, 29; 3:6, 13, 22.\f*
\q1 \v 10 If anyone is for captivity, into captivity he goes;
\q1 if anyone is to be killed with the sword, with the sword he is to be killed.
\q1 Here is the perseverance and the faith of the saints.\f + \fr 13:10 \ft Lit. “if anyone for captivity, into captivity he goes; if anyone with the sword to be killed, he with the sword to be killed”. Cf. Jer 15:2; 43:11 [LXX 50:11]. The SBLGNT is followed, with Codex Alexandrinus. Variants: codices Sinaiticus and Ephraemi, the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “if anyone kills with the sword, he must be killed with the sword”; in the first clause, the Textus Receptus reads “if anyone gathers captives, into captivity he goes”, and the Byzantine text, “if anyone has captivity, he goes”. “Perseverance”: Gr. ὑπομονή (cf. 1:9; 14:12).\f*
\s1 The beast that rises from the earth
\p \v 11 And I saw another beast coming up out of the earth, and it had two horns like those of a lamb, and it spoke like a dragon.\f + \fr 13:11 \ft “Lamb”: Gr. ἀρνίον, the same term as in 5:6; here in a comparison, without the article, and so in lowercase. “Out of the earth”: in contrast with “out of the sea” (13:1).\f* \v 12 It exercises all the authority of the first beast in its presence, and makes the earth and those who dwell in it worship the first beast, whose mortal wound was healed.\f + \fr 13:12 \ft “Exercises”: Gr. ποιεῖ, lit. “does”. “In its presence”: Gr. ἐνώπιον αὐτοῦ.\f* \v 13 It performs great signs, so that it even makes fire come down from heaven to the earth in the presence of men.\f + \fr 13:13 \ft “Signs”: Gr. σημεῖα (cf. 16:14; 19:20). “Fire… from heaven”: cf. 1 Kgs 18:38; 2 Kgs 1:10.\f* \v 14 And it deceives those who dwell on the earth because of the signs that it was granted to perform in the presence of the beast, telling those who dwell on the earth to make an image to the beast that has the wound of the sword and came to life.\f + \fr 13:14 \ft “Deceives”: Gr. πλανάω (cf. 12:9; 19:20). “Image”: Gr. εἰκών (cf. Dan 3:1–7). “That has”: Gr. ὅς, masculine; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read ὅ, neuter. “Came to life”: Gr. ἔζησεν (cf. 2:8; 20:4).\f*
\p \v 15 It was granted to give breath to the image of the beast, so that the image of the beast would even speak and would cause all who would not worship the image of the beast to be killed.\f + \fr 13:15 \ft “Breath”: Gr. πνεῦμα (cf. 11:11). “All who would not worship… to be killed”: cf. Dan 3:6, 15.\f* \v 16 It causes all, the small and the great, and the rich and the poor, and the free and the slaves, to be given a mark on their right hand or on their forehead,\f + \fr 13:16 \ft “The small and the great”: cf. 11:18; 19:5, 18; 20:12. “Slaves”: Gr. δοῦλοι, beside “free” (cf. 6:15). “Mark”: Gr. χάραγμα (cf. 14:9, 11; 16:2; 19:20; 20:4). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “that he give them”, singular. “Right hand… forehead”: cf. 7:3; 14:1; Deut 6:8.\f* \v 17 and that no one may be able to buy or sell except the one who has the mark, the name of the beast or the number of its name.\f + \fr 13:17 \ft “Buy or sell”: Gr. ἀγοράζω (cf. 3:18; 5:9). “The number of its name”: cf. 15:2. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “or the name”.\f*
\p \v 18 Here is wisdom. Let the one who has understanding calculate the number of the beast, for it is the number of a man; and its number is six hundred and sixty-six.\f + \fr 13:18 \ft “Understanding”: Gr. νοῦς (cf. 17:9). “Calculate”: Gr. ψηφισάτω, “count, calculate”. “The number of a man”: Gr. ἀριθμὸς ἀνθρώπου, without the article; also “a human number”. “Six hundred and sixty-six”: so Papyrus 47 and codices Sinaiticus and Alexandrinus. Variant: Codex Ephraemi and Papyrus 115 read “six hundred and sixteen”.\f*
\c 14
\s1 The Lamb and the one hundred and forty-four thousand
\p \v 1 And I saw, and behold, the Lamb standing on Mount Zion, and with Him one hundred and forty-four thousand, who had His name and the name of His Father written on their foreheads.\f + \fr 14:1 \ft “Mount Zion”: cf. Ps 2:6; Isa 24:23; Joel 2:32 (Heb. 3:5); Heb 12:22. “His name and the name of His Father”: cf. 3:12; 7:3; 22:4; the Textus Receptus omits “His name and”. Variant: the Byzantine text reads “a number of one hundred and forty-four thousand”; the Textus Receptus reads “a Lamb”.\f*
\p \v 2 And I heard a voice from heaven, like the voice of many waters and like the voice of loud thunder; and the voice that I heard was like that of harpists playing their harps.\f + \fr 14:2 \ft “Voice of many waters”: cf. 1:15; 19:6; Ezek 43:2. “Harpists playing their harps”: Gr. κιθαρῳδῶν κιθαριζόντων ἐν ταῖς κιθάραις (cf. 5:8; 15:2).\f* \v 3 They sing as it were a new song before the throne, and before the four living creatures and the elders; and no one could learn the song except the one hundred and forty-four thousand, those who had been purchased from the earth.\f + \fr 14:3 \ft “As it were a new song”: Gr. ὡς ᾠδὴν καινήν (cf. 5:9; 15:3); the Byzantine text omits “as it were”. “Purchased”: Gr. ἀγοράζω (cf. 5:9).\f* \v 4 These are the ones who were not defiled with women, for they are virgins. These are the ones who follow the Lamb wherever He goes. These were purchased from among men as firstfruits to God and to the Lamb,\f + \fr 14:4 \ft “Virgins”: Gr. παρθένοι, here applied to men (οὗτοι) (cf. 2 Cor 11:2). “Follow the Lamb”: cf. John 10:4, 27. “Firstfruits”: Gr. ἀπαρχή (cf. Jer 2:3; Jas 1:18). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “these were purchased by Jesus”.\f* \v 5 and in their mouth no lie was found; they are blameless.\f + \fr 14:5 \ft “Lie”: Gr. ψεῦδος; the Textus Receptus reads “deceit” and adds “before the throne of God”. Cf. Zeph 3:13; Isa 53:9. “Blameless”: Gr. ἄμωμοι, “without blemish” (cf. Ex 29:1; 1 Pet 1:19); the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “for they are blameless”.\f*
\s1 The messages of the three angels
\p \v 6 And I saw another angel flying in midheaven, having the eternal gospel to proclaim to those who sit on the earth, and to every nation, and tribe, and tongue, and people,\f + \fr 14:6 \ft “Flying in midheaven”: cf. 8:13. “The eternal gospel”: Gr. εὐαγγέλιον αἰώνιον, without the article in the Greek. “Proclaim”: Gr. εὐαγγελίσαι, “announce the good news” (cf. 10:7), here with “gospel” as its object. “Sit”: Gr. καθημένους, lit. “seated”; Papyrus 115, Codex Alexandrinus and the Textus Receptus read “dwell”. “Every nation, and tribe, and tongue, and people”: cf. 5:9; 13:7. Variant: the Byzantine text omits “another”.\f* \v 7 saying with a loud voice:
\q1 Fear God and give Him glory,
\q1 because the hour of His judgment has come;
\q1 and worship Him who made heaven, and earth,
\q1 and the sea, and the springs of waters.\f + \fr 14:7 \ft “Fear God and give Him glory”: cf. 11:13; 15:4; Eccl 12:13; Jer 13:16; the Byzantine text reads “Fear the Lord”. “Judgment”: Gr. κρίσις (cf. 16:7; 18:10; 19:2; Dan 7:10, 22, 26). “Him who made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and the springs of waters”: cf. Ex 20:11; Ps 146:6 [LXX 145:6]; Acts 14:15; Rev 10:6; the SBLGNT has no article before “sea” and “springs”; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “the sea”.\f*
\p \v 8 And another angel, a second, followed, saying:
\q1 Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great,
\q1 which made all the nations drink
\q1 of the wine of the fury of her prostitution.\f + \fr 14:8 \ft “Another angel, a second”: Gr. ἄλλος δεύτερος ἄγγελος. “Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great”: cf. 18:2; Isa 21:9; Jer 51:7–8 [LXX 28:7–8]; Dan 4:30 (Heb. 4:27). “Fury”: Gr. θυμός, also “passion” (cf. 12:12; 18:3). “Made… drink”: Gr. πεπότικεν, perfect. Variants: the Textus Receptus omits “a second” and reads “Babylon the city, the great, because she made… drink”; the Byzantine text has “fallen” only once.\f*
\p \v 9 And another angel, a third, followed them, saying with a loud voice: If anyone worships the beast and its image, and receives the mark on his forehead or on his hand,\f + \fr 14:9 \ft “Worships… receives”: presents in the Greek. “The mark”: Gr. χάραγμα, without the article in the Greek; it is the mark of the beast (cf. 13:16–17; 14:11).\f* \v 10 he too will drink of the wine of the fury of God, prepared undiluted in the cup of His wrath; and he will be tormented with fire and sulfur before holy angels and before the Lamb.\f + \fr 14:10 \ft “Prepared undiluted”: Gr. κεκερασμένου ἀκράτου, lit. “mixed unmixed”. “Holy angels”: without the article; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “the holy angels”. “Cup of His wrath”: cf. 16:19; Ps 75:8 (Heb. 75:9) [LXX 74:9]; Isa 51:17; Jer 25:15 [LXX 32:1]. “Fire and sulfur”: cf. 19:20; 20:10; 21:8; Gen 19:24.\f* \v 11 And the smoke of their torment goes up forever and ever; and they have no rest, day and night, those who worship the beast and its image, and whoever receives the mark of its name.\f + \fr 14:11 \ft “Forever and ever”: Gr. εἰς αἰῶνας αἰώνων, without articles (cf. 1:6). Cf. Isa 34:9–10; Jude 7. “Rest”: Gr. ἀνάπαυσις (cf. 4:8).\f* \v 12 Here is the perseverance of the saints, those who keep the commandments of God and the faith of Jesus.\f + \fr 14:12 \ft “Perseverance”: Gr. ὑπομονή (cf. 1:9; 13:10). “Those who keep the commandments of God”: cf. 12:17. “The faith of Jesus”: Gr. τὴν πίστιν Ἰησοῦ; alternative reading: “faith in Jesus” (cf. 2:13). Variant: the Textus Receptus repeats “here”: “here are those who keep”.\f*
\p \v 13 And I heard a voice from heaven, saying: Write:
\q1 Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from now on.
\q1 Yes, says the Spirit, so that they may rest from their labors,
\q1 for their works follow them.\f + \fr 14:13 \ft “Write”: cf. 1:11, 19; 19:9; 21:5. “Blessed”: cf. 1:3; 16:15; 19:9; 20:6; 22:7, 14. “From now on”: Gr. ἀπ’ ἄρτι; alternative reading: it goes with what follows, “From now on, yes, says the Spirit”. “Rest”: Gr. ἀναπαήσονται (cf. 6:11). “Labors”: Gr. κόποι (cf. 2:2). “For their works follow them”: lit. “follow with them”. Variants: the Textus Receptus reads “saying to me”; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “and their works”.\f*
\s1 The harvest of the earth
\p \v 14 And I saw, and behold, a white cloud, and on the cloud One seated, like a Son of Man, having on His head a golden crown and in His hand a sharp sickle.\f + \fr 14:14 \ft “White cloud”: cf. 1:7; Matt 24:30. “One… like a Son of Man”: Gr. ὅμοιον υἱὸν ἀνθρώπου (cf. 1:13; Dan 7:13). The capital identifies the figure with the One “like a Son of Man” of 1:13, who comes “with the clouds” (1:7); here He also wears a golden crown. Another reading sees here an angel, since the order to reap comes from “another angel” (14:15). “Crown”: Gr. στέφανος. “Sickle”: Gr. δρέπανον.\f* \v 15 And another angel came out of the sanctuary, crying out with a loud voice to Him who sat on the cloud: Thrust in Your sickle and reap, because the hour to reap has come, because the harvest of the earth is ripe.\f + \fr 14:15 \ft “Sanctuary”: Gr. ναός (cf. 11:19). “Thrust in”: Gr. πέμψον, lit. “send”. “The harvest… is ripe”: Gr. ἐξηράνθη, lit. “has dried”. Cf. Joel 3:13 (Heb. 4:13); Matt 13:30, 39; Mark 4:29. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “the hour has come for You”.\f* \v 16 Then He who sat on the cloud cast His sickle on the earth, and the earth was reaped.
\p \v 17 And another angel came out of the sanctuary that is in heaven, he too having a sharp sickle.\f + \fr 14:17 \ft “He too”: Gr. καὶ αὐτός.\f* \v 18 And another angel, the one who had authority over the fire, came out from the altar and called with a loud voice to the one who had the sharp sickle, saying: Thrust in your sharp sickle and gather the clusters of the vine of the earth, because its grapes are ripe.\f + \fr 14:18 \ft “Came out from the altar”: the verb is implied in the SBLGNT (cf. 14:17); the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus have ἐξῆλθεν and read “with a loud cry”. “Authority over the fire”: cf. 8:3–5. “Gather”: Gr. τρύγησον, “harvest grapes”. Cf. Joel 3:13 (Heb. 4:13).\f* \v 19 Then the angel cast his sickle to the earth, gathered the vine of the earth, and cast it into the great winepress of the fury of God.\f + \fr 14:19 \ft “Winepress of the fury of God”: cf. 19:15; Isa 63:1–6; Lam 1:15.\f* \v 20 The winepress was trodden outside the city, and blood came out of the winepress up to the bridles of the horses, for one thousand six hundred stadia.\f + \fr 14:20 \ft Cf. Joel 3:12–14 (Heb. 4:12–14). “One thousand six hundred stadia”: about 300 kilometers (about 185 miles); one stadion, about 185 meters (607 feet).\f*
\c 15
\s1 The song of Moses and of the Lamb
\p \v 1 And I saw another sign in heaven, great and marvelous: seven angels who had the seven last plagues, because in them the fury of God was completed.\f + \fr 15:1 \ft “Sign”: Gr. σημεῖον (cf. 12:1, 3). “The seven last plagues”: Gr. πληγὰς ἑπτὰ τὰς ἐσχάτας (cf. 16:1; 21:9; Lev 26:21). “Was completed”: Gr. ἐτελέσθη (cf. 10:7). “Fury”: Gr. θυμός (cf. 14:10).\f*
\p \v 2 And I saw as it were a sea of glass mixed with fire, and the conquerors of the beast, and of its image, and of the number of its name, standing on the sea of glass, having harps of God.\f + \fr 15:2 \ft “As it were a sea of glass”: cf. 4:6. “The conquerors of the beast”: Gr. τοὺς νικῶντας ἐκ τοῦ θηρίου, lit. “those who conquer from the beast”. “Number of its name”: cf. 13:17–18; the Textus Receptus adds “and of its mark”. “Standing on”: Gr. ἐπί with the accusative, also “beside”. “Harps of God”: cf. 5:8; 14:2.\f* \v 3 They sing the song of Moses, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb, saying:
\q1 Great and marvelous are Your works,
\q1 O Lord God, the Almighty;
\q1 just and true are Your ways,
\q1 O King of the nations.\f + \fr 15:3 \ft “The song of Moses”: cf. Ex 15:1–18; Deut 31:30–32:43. “Servant of God”: cf. Num 12:7; Josh 1:2. “Great and marvelous are Your works”: cf. Ps 111:2 [LXX 110:2]; 139:14 [LXX 138:14]. “Just and true are Your ways”: cf. Deut 32:4; Ps 145:17 [LXX 144:17]. “King of the nations”: Gr. ὁ βασιλεὺς τῶν ἐθνῶν, following Codex Alexandrinus and the Byzantine text (cf. 15:4; Jer 10:7); the SBLGNT follows Papyrus 47, the original text of Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Ephraemi, which read “King of the ages” (cf. 1 Tim 1:17). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “King of the saints”.\f*
\q1 \v 4 Who will not fear You, O Lord,
\q1 and glorify Your name?
\q1 For You alone are holy;
\q1 for all the nations will come
\q1 and worship before You,
\q1 for Your righteous acts have been made manifest.\f + \fr 15:4 \ft “Who will not fear You”: Gr. οὐ μή, an emphatic negation, which also governs “glorify”; cf. Jer 10:7 (not in the LXX). “You” is not in the SBLGNT; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read σε. “Holy”: Gr. ὅσιος (cf. 16:5); the Byzantine text reads ἅγιος. “All the nations will come and worship”: cf. Ps 86:9 [LXX 85:9]; Isa 2:2–3; 66:23. “Righteous acts”: Gr. δικαιώματα (cf. 19:8). “Have been made manifest”: Gr. ἐφανερώθησαν.\f*
\s1 The seven angels with the seven plagues
\p \v 5 And after these things I saw, and the sanctuary of the tabernacle of the testimony was opened in heaven,\f + \fr 15:5 \ft “The sanctuary of the tabernacle of the testimony”: Gr. ὁ ναὸς τῆς σκηνῆς τοῦ μαρτυρίου (cf. 11:19; Ex 38:21 [LXX 37:19]; Num 1:50; Acts 7:44). Variant: the Textus Receptus adds “and behold”.\f* \v 6 and the seven angels who had the seven plagues came out of the sanctuary, clothed in pure and bright linen, and girded at the chest with golden sashes.\f + \fr 15:6 \ft “Pure and bright linen”: Gr. λίνον καθαρὸν λαμπρόν (cf. 19:8, 14); codices Alexandrinus and Ephraemi read λίθον, “stone” (cf. Ezek 28:13). “Girded at the chest with golden sashes”: cf. 1:13; Dan 10:5.\f* \v 7 Then one of the four living creatures gave the seven angels seven golden bowls full of the fury of God, who lives forever and ever.\f + \fr 15:7 \ft “Bowls”: Gr. φιάλας (cf. 5:8; 16:1). “Who lives forever and ever”: cf. 4:9–10; 10:6.\f* \v 8 The sanctuary was filled with smoke from the glory of God and from His power; and no one could enter the sanctuary until the seven plagues of the seven angels were completed.\f + \fr 15:8 \ft “From the glory”: Gr. ἐκ τῆς δόξης. “The sanctuary was filled with smoke”: cf. Ex 40:34–35 [LXX 40:28–29]; 1 Kgs 8:10–11; 2 Chr 5:13–14; Isa 6:4. “Were completed”: Gr. τελεσθῶσιν (cf. 15:1).\f*
\c 16
\s1 The seven bowls of the wrath of God
\p \v 1 And I heard a loud voice from the sanctuary, saying to the seven angels: Go and pour out on the earth the seven bowls of the fury of God.\f + \fr 16:1 \ft “A loud voice from the sanctuary”: Gr. ναός (cf. 15:5–8). “Pour out”: Gr. ἐκχέετε. “Bowls of the fury of God”: cf. 15:1, 7. Cf. Ex 7–10.\f*
\p \v 2 And the first went and poured out his bowl on the earth: a bad and malignant sore came upon the men who had the mark of the beast and who worshiped its image.\f + \fr 16:2 \ft “A bad and malignant sore”: Gr. ἕλκος κακὸν καὶ πονηρόν (cf. Ex 9:9–11; Deut 28:35). “The mark of the beast… its image”: cf. 13:15–17; 14:9–11.\f*
\p \v 3 And the second poured out his bowl into the sea: it became blood like that of a dead man, and every living soul that was in the sea died.\f + \fr 16:3 \ft “It became blood”: lit. “there came blood”. “Blood like that of a dead man”: cf. 8:8–9; Ex 7:17–21. “Every living soul”: Gr. πᾶσα ψυχὴ ζωῆς, lit. “every soul of life” (cf. Gen 1:21); the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read ψυχὴ ζῶσα.\f*
\p \v 4 And the third poured out his bowl into the rivers and the springs of waters: they became blood.\f + \fr 16:4 \ft “They became blood”: lit. “there came blood” (cf. Ex 7:19–21; Ps 78:44 [LXX 77:44]).\f* \v 5 And I heard the angel of the waters saying: Just are You, Who Is, and Who Was, the Holy One, because You have judged these things;\f + \fr 16:5 \ft “The angel of the waters”: cf. 7:1; 14:18. “Just are You”: cf. 15:3; Deut 32:4; Ps 119:137 [LXX 118:137]. “Who Is, and Who Was”: cf. 1:4, 8; 11:17. “The Holy One”: Gr. ὁ ὅσιος (cf. 15:4); the Textus Receptus reads “Just are You, O Lord, who is, and who was, and who shall be”.\f* \v 6 because they poured out the blood of saints and of prophets, and You have given them blood to drink; they deserve it.\f + \fr 16:6 \ft “Blood of saints and of prophets”: cf. 17:6; 18:24. “You have given them blood to drink”: cf. Isa 49:26. “They deserve it”: Gr. ἄξιοί εἰσιν, lit. “they are worthy” (cf. 3:4); the Textus Receptus reads “for they are worthy”.\f* \v 7 And I heard the altar saying: Yes, O Lord God, the Almighty, true and just are Your judgments.\f + \fr 16:7 \ft “I heard the altar saying”: cf. 6:9–10; 8:3–5; 9:13; the Textus Receptus reads “I heard another, from the altar, saying”. “Yes”: Gr. ναί (cf. 1:7). “True and just are Your judgments”: Gr. κρίσεις (cf. 15:3; 19:2).\f*
\p \v 8 And the fourth poured out his bowl on the sun: it was granted to it to scorch men with fire.\f + \fr 16:8 \ft “Scorch”: Gr. καυματίσαι (cf. 7:16).\f* \v 9 Men were scorched with great heat, and they blasphemed the name of God, who has authority over these plagues, and they did not repent to give Him glory.\f + \fr 16:9 \ft “Heat”: Gr. καῦμα (cf. 7:16). “Blasphemed”: cf. 13:6; 16:11, 21. “Did not repent”: cf. 2:21; 9:20–21. “Give Him glory”: cf. 11:13; 14:7.\f*
\p \v 10 And the fifth poured out his bowl on the throne of the beast: its kingdom was darkened, and they gnawed their tongues because of the pain,\f + \fr 16:10 \ft “The throne of the beast”: cf. 2:13; 13:2. “Was darkened”: Gr. ἐσκοτωμένη, perfect participle (cf. Ex 10:21–23; Joel 2:31 (Heb. 3:4)).\f* \v 11 and they blasphemed the God of heaven because of their pains and their sores, and they did not repent of their works.\f + \fr 16:11 \ft “The God of heaven”: cf. 11:13; Dan 2:18–19, 44. “Sores”: cf. 16:2.\f*
\p \v 12 And the sixth poured out his bowl on the great river Euphrates: its water dried up, so that the way of the kings from the rising of the sun might be prepared.\f + \fr 16:12 \ft “The great river Euphrates”: cf. 9:14. “Its water dried up”: cf. Isa 11:15; 44:27; Jer 50:38 [LXX 27:38]; 51:36 [LXX 28:36]. “The kings from the rising of the sun”: cf. 7:2; Isa 41:2, 25; 46:11; the Textus Receptus reads “risings” in the plural.\f* \v 13 And I saw coming out of the mouth of the dragon, and out of the mouth of the beast, and out of the mouth of the false prophet, three unclean spirits like frogs;\f + \fr 16:13 \ft “The false prophet”: Gr. ψευδοπροφήτης (cf. 13:11–14; 19:20; 20:10). “I saw coming out of the mouth”: lit. “I saw out of the mouth”. “Unclean spirits”: Gr. πνεύματα ἀκάθαρτα. “Like frogs”: Gr. ὡς βάτραχοι (cf. Ex 8:2–7 (Heb. 7:27–8:3)); the Textus Receptus reads ὅμοια βατράχοις, “resembling frogs”.\f* \v 14 for they are spirits of demons, performing signs, who go out to the kings of the whole world, to gather them for the war of the great day of God, the Almighty —\f + \fr 16:14 \ft “Demons”: Gr. δαιμόνια (cf. 9:20). “Signs”: Gr. σημεῖα (cf. 13:13–14; 19:20). “Whole world”: Gr. οἰκουμένη ὅλη (cf. 3:10; 12:9). “Gather them for the war”: cf. 19:19; 20:8. “The great day of God”: cf. 6:17; Joel 2:11; 2:31 (Heb. 3:4); Zeph 1:14. Variants: the Textus Receptus reads “the kings of the earth and of the whole world”; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “of that great day”. The dash marks the parenthesis of v. 15, as in the Greek.\f* \v 15 Behold, I am coming like a thief. Blessed is the one who watches and keeps his garments, so that he may not walk naked and others not see his shame —\f + \fr 16:15 \ft “Behold, I am coming like a thief”: cf. 3:3; Matt 24:43; Luke 12:39; 1 Thess 5:2; 2 Pet 3:10. “Blessed”: cf. 1:3; 14:13. “Watches”: Gr. γρηγορέω (cf. 3:2–3). “Garments”: Gr. ἱμάτια (cf. 3:4–5, 18). “Shame”: Gr. ἀσχημοσύνη, lit. “indecency”, nakedness exposed (cf. 3:18, where “shame” renders αἰσχύνη).\f* \v 16 And they gathered them to the place that in Hebrew is called Armageddon.\f + \fr 16:16 \ft “They gathered”: Gr. συνήγαγεν, singular with a neuter plural subject, the spirits (16:13–14); alternative reading: “And he gathered them”. “Armageddon”: Gr. Ἁρμαγεδών, from the Hebrew, generally understood as “mountain of Megiddo” (cf. Judg 5:19; 2 Kgs 23:29; Zech 12:11); alternative reading: “mountain of assembly” (cf. Isa 14:13); the Textus Receptus reads Ἁρμαγεδδών, the source of the familiar English spelling.\f*
\p \v 17 And the seventh poured out his bowl upon the air: a loud voice came out of the sanctuary, from the throne, saying: It is done!\f + \fr 16:17 \ft “Upon the air”: the Textus Receptus reads “into the air”. “Out of the sanctuary, from the throne”: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus add “of heaven” after “sanctuary”. “It is done”: Gr. γέγονεν, perfect (cf. 21:6; also 10:7; 15:1).\f* \v 18 And there were flashes of lightning, and voices, and thunders, and there was a great earthquake, such as there had never been since men came to be on the earth, so great an earthquake as this.\f + \fr 16:18 \ft “Flashes of lightning, and voices, and thunders”: cf. 4:5; 8:5; 11:19. “Such as there had never been”: cf. Dan 12:1; Matt 24:21. “Since men came to be”: some manuscripts read “man”, singular. “So great an earthquake as this”: Gr. τηλικοῦτος σεισμὸς οὕτω μέγας, lit. “such an earthquake, so great”.\f* \v 19 And the great city was split into three parts, and the cities of the nations fell; and Babylon the great was remembered before God, to give her the cup of the wine of the fury of His wrath.\f + \fr 16:19 \ft “The great city”: cf. 11:8; 17:18; 18:10. “Was split into three parts”: lit. “came to be in three parts”. “Was remembered before God”: Gr. ἐμνήσθη ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ (cf. 18:5). “The cup of the wine of the fury of His wrath”: cf. 14:8, 10; 18:3; Jer 25:15 [LXX 32:1].\f* \v 20 And every island fled away, and the mountains were not found.\f + \fr 16:20 \ft Cf. 6:14; 20:11.\f* \v 21 And great hail, about the weight of a talent, comes down from heaven upon men; and men blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail, for its plague is exceedingly great.\f + \fr 16:21 \ft “Hail”: cf. 8:7; 11:19; Ex 9:23–25. “About the weight of a talent”: Gr. ὡς ταλαντιαία, “weighing a talent”, a measure of weight of about 30 to 40 kilograms (65 to 90 pounds). “Comes down”: present in the Greek. “Plague”: Gr. πληγή (cf. 9:18; 15:1).\f*
\c 17
\s1 The great prostitute and the beast
\p \v 1 And one of the seven angels who had the seven bowls came and spoke with me, saying: Come, I will show you the judgment of the great prostitute who is seated on many waters,\f + \fr 17:1 \ft “One of the seven angels who had the seven bowls”: cf. 15:7; 21:9. “Come”: Gr. δεῦρο. “Judgment”: Gr. κρίμα (cf. 18:20; 20:4). “Prostitute”: Gr. πόρνη (cf. 17:15–16; 19:2). “Seated on many waters”: cf. 17:15; Jer 51:13 [LXX 28:13].\f* \v 2 with whom the kings of the earth prostituted themselves, and those who dwell on the earth became drunk with the wine of her prostitution.\f + \fr 17:2 \ft “Prostituted themselves”: Gr. πορνεύω (cf. 2:14, 20; 18:3, 9). “Became drunk with the wine of her prostitution”: cf. 14:8; 18:3; Jer 51:7 [LXX 28:7].\f*
\p \v 3 And he carried me away in the Spirit into a wilderness; and I saw a woman seated on a scarlet beast, full of names of blasphemy, having seven heads and ten horns.\f + \fr 17:3 \ft “Carried me away in the Spirit”: Gr. ἀπήνεγκέν με… ἐν πνεύματι (cf. 1:10; 4:2; 21:10; Ezek 3:12, 14). “Scarlet”: Gr. κόκκινος (cf. 17:4; 18:12, 16). “Names of blasphemy”: cf. 13:1, 5–6. “Seven heads and ten horns”: cf. 12:3; 13:1.\f* \v 4 The woman was clothed in purple and scarlet, and adorned with gold, and precious stones, and pearls, having in her hand a golden cup full of abominations and of the impurities of her prostitution,\f + \fr 17:4 \ft “Clothed”: Gr. περιβάλλω (cf. 3:5). “Purple and scarlet”: cf. 18:16; Ex 28:5–6. “Adorned with gold”: lit. “gilded with gold”. “Golden cup”: cf. Jer 51:7 [LXX 28:7]. “Abominations”: Gr. βδελύγματα (cf. 17:5; 21:27; Dan 9:27; 11:31). “Impurities”: Gr. τὰ ἀκάθαρτα; the Textus Receptus reads “of the impurity”.\f* \v 5 and on her forehead a name written: Mystery, Babylon the great, the mother of the prostitutes and of the abominations of the earth.\f + \fr 17:5 \ft “On her forehead a name written”: cf. 13:16; 14:1, 9; 22:4. “Mystery”: Gr. μυστήριον, part of the name or a description of it (cf. 17:7). “Babylon the great”: cf. 14:8; 16:19; 18:2; Dan 4:30 (Heb. 4:27). “The mother of the prostitutes”: cf. Ezek 16:44–45.\f* \v 6 And I saw the woman drunk with the blood of the saints and with the blood of the witnesses of Jesus. When I saw her, I marveled with great amazement.\f + \fr 17:6 \ft “Drunk with the blood of the saints”: cf. 6:10; 16:6; 18:24. “Witnesses”: Gr. μάρτυρες (cf. 2:13; 11:3). “I marveled with great amazement”: Gr. ἐθαύμασα… θαῦμα μέγα, lit. “I marveled with a great marvel”.\f*
\s1 The mystery of the woman and of the beast
\p \v 7 Then the angel said to me: Why did you marvel? I will tell you the mystery of the woman and of the beast that carries her, which has the seven heads and the ten horns.\f + \fr 17:7 \ft “Mystery”: cf. 1:20; 10:7. “Carries”: Gr. βαστάζω.\f* \v 8 The beast that you saw was, and is not, and is about to come up out of the abyss, and goes to destruction; and those who dwell on the earth, whose name is not written in the book of life from the foundation of the world, will marvel when they see the beast, that it was, and is not, and will come.\f + \fr 17:8 \ft “Was, and is not, and is about to come up”: cf. 1:4, 8; 4:8; 11:7. “Abyss”: cf. 9:1; 11:7. “Destruction”: Gr. ἀπώλεια (cf. 9:11). “Whose name is not written in the book of life from the foundation of the world”: cf. 13:8. “That it was”: Gr. ὅτι, object of “see”. “And will come”: Gr. καὶ παρέσται, from πάρειμι, lit. “and will be present”, not the verb ἔρχομαι of 1:4, 8; the Textus Receptus reads “and yet is”. Variants: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “and to go to destruction” and “whose names”.\f*
\p \v 9 Here is the understanding that has wisdom. The seven heads are seven mountains, on which the woman is seated. And they are seven kings:\f + \fr 17:9 \ft “Here is the understanding”: cf. 13:18. “Seven mountains”: Gr. ὄρη. “And they are seven kings”: alternative reading: “and there are seven kings”; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus place the phrase in 17:10.\f* \v 10 five have fallen, one is, the other has not yet come; and when he comes, he must remain a little while.\f + \fr 17:10 \ft “Have fallen”: Gr. ἔπεσαν. “A little while”: Gr. ὀλίγον.\f* \v 11 And the beast that was and is not, he also is the eighth, and is of the seven, and goes to destruction.\f + \fr 17:11 \ft “Is the eighth, and is of the seven”: Gr. ὄγδοός ἐστιν καὶ ἐκ τῶν ἑπτά ἐστιν. “He also”: Gr. καὶ αὐτός, masculine, although “beast” (θηρίον) is neuter. “Is of the seven”: also “comes from the seven” or “is one of the seven”.\f*
\p \v 12 And the ten horns that you saw are ten kings, who have not yet received a kingdom, but receive authority as kings for one hour, with the beast.\f + \fr 17:12 \ft “Ten horns… ten kings”: cf. 13:1; Dan 7:7, 20, 24. “One hour”: cf. 18:10, 17, 19.\f* \v 13 These have one purpose, and they give their power and authority to the beast.\f + \fr 17:13 \ft “One purpose”: Gr. μίαν γνώμην, lit. “one opinion”.\f* \v 14 These will make war against the Lamb, and the Lamb will conquer them, because He is Lord of lords and King of kings; and those who are with Him are called, and chosen, and faithful.\f + \fr 17:14 \ft “Will make war against the Lamb”: cf. 16:14; 19:19. “Lord of lords and King of kings”: cf. 19:16; Deut 10:17; Dan 2:47; 1 Tim 6:15. “And those who are with Him are called, and chosen, and faithful”: the Greek has no verb; alternative reading: “and those with Him, called, and chosen, and faithful, will also conquer them” (cf. 2:10; 19:9, 14; Matt 22:14). “Lord of lords”: without articles in the Greek.\f*
\p \v 15 And he says to me: The waters that you saw, where the prostitute is seated, are peoples, and multitudes, and nations, and tongues.\f + \fr 17:15 \ft “Peoples, and multitudes, and nations, and tongues”: cf. 5:9; 7:9; 10:11; 13:7; 14:6.\f* \v 16 And the ten horns that you saw, and the beast, these will hate the prostitute, and will make her desolate and naked, and will eat her flesh, and will burn her with fire.\f + \fr 17:16 \ft “And the beast”: the Textus Receptus reads “that you saw upon the beast”. “Will hate the prostitute”: cf. Ezek 16:37–41; 23:25–29. “Will eat her flesh”: cf. 19:18; 2 Kgs 9:36. “Will burn her with fire”: cf. 18:8; Lev 21:9.\f* \v 17 For God has put it into their hearts to carry out His purpose, and to have one purpose, and to give their kingdom to the beast, until the words of God are fulfilled.\f + \fr 17:17 \ft “Has put it into their hearts”: Gr. ἔδωκεν εἰς τὰς καρδίας, lit. “gave into their hearts”. “Carry out”: Gr. ποιῆσαι, “do”. “His purpose”: Gr. αὐτοῦ, God’s. “Their kingdom”: Gr. αὐτῶν, that of the ten kings. “Purpose”: Gr. γνώμη (cf. 17:13). “Are fulfilled”: Gr. τελεσθήσονται (cf. 10:7; 15:1). “Words of God”: Gr. οἱ λόγοι τοῦ θεοῦ; the Textus Receptus reads τὰ ῥήματα.\f* \v 18 And the woman that you saw is the great city that reigns over the kings of the earth.\f + \fr 17:18 \ft “The great city”: cf. 11:8; 16:19; 18:10. “That reigns”: Gr. ἡ ἔχουσα βασιλείαν, lit. “that has a kingdom”.\f*
\c 18
\s1 The fall of Babylon
\p \v 1 After these things I saw another angel coming down from heaven, having great authority, and the earth was illuminated by his glory.\f + \fr 18:1 \ft “Another angel”: cf. 10:1; 14:6, 8. “The earth was illuminated by his glory”: cf. Ezek 43:2.\f* \v 2 He cried out with a mighty voice, saying:
\q1 Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great,
\q1 and has become a dwelling place of demons,
\q1 and a prison of every unclean spirit,
\q1 and a prison of every unclean bird,
\q1 and a prison of every unclean and hated beast,\f + \fr 18:2 \ft “Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great”: cf. 14:8; Isa 21:9; Jer 51:8 [LXX 28:8]. “A dwelling place of demons… a prison”: Gr. φυλακή, also “haunt, lair” (cf. Isa 13:21–22; 34:11–15; Jer 50:39 [LXX 27:39]; 51:37 [LXX 28:37]). “Mighty voice”: Gr. ἰσχυρᾷ φωνῇ; the Textus Receptus reads “mightily, with a loud voice”. “Beast”: Gr. θηρίον, here the wild animal (cf. 6:8). “Hated”: Gr. μεμισημένου, participle. “And a prison of every unclean and hated beast”: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus omit “a prison of every unclean… beast”, reading “every unclean and hated bird”. Variant: the Byzantine text has “fallen” only once.\f*
\q1 \v 3 because all the nations have drunk of the wine of the fury of her prostitution,
\q1 and the kings of the earth prostituted themselves with her,
\q1 and the merchants of the earth grew rich from the power of her luxury.\f + \fr 18:3 \ft “Have drunk”: Gr. πέπωκαν (the Textus Receptus πέπωκε), following the Latin Vulgate, some manuscripts and the Textus Receptus (cf. 14:8; 17:2); the SBLGNT follows codices Sinaiticus, Alexandrinus and Ephraemi, which read πέπτωκαν, and the Byzantine text, which reads πεπτώκασιν, both “have fallen”. “Merchants”: Gr. ἔμποροι (cf. Ezek 27:12–36). “Luxury”: Gr. στρῆνος, “sensual excess, opulence”.\f*
\s1 Come out of her, My people
\p \v 4 And I heard another voice from heaven, saying: Come out of her, My people, so that you do not share in her sins and so that you do not receive any of her plagues;\f + \fr 18:4 \ft “Come out of her, My people… share… receive”: in the Greek, all the verbs are plural (ἐξέλθατε… συγκοινωνήσητε… λάβητε). Codex Ephraemi and the Byzantine text read ἔξελθε, singular (cf. Isa 48:20; 52:11; Jer 50:8 [LXX 27:8]; 51:6 [LXX 28:6]; 51:45 (not in the LXX); 2 Cor 6:17).\f* \v 5 for her sins have piled up to heaven, and God has remembered her iniquities.\f + \fr 18:5 \ft “Have piled up”: Gr. ἐκολλήθησαν, lit. “have stuck together, have been joined” (cf. Gen 18:20; Jer 51:9 [LXX 28:9]). “Has remembered”: cf. 16:19.\f* \v 6 Repay her as she herself repaid, and give her double according to her works; in the cup in which she mixed, mix her double.\f + \fr 18:6 \ft “Repay her as she herself repaid”: cf. Ps 137:8 [LXX 136:8]; Jer 50:15, 29 [LXX 27:15, 29]. “Give her double”: Gr. διπλώσατε τὰ διπλᾶ, lit. “double the double” (cf. Isa 40:2; Jer 16:18); the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “double to her”. “Mixed… mix”: Gr. κεράννυμι (cf. 14:10, “prepared”). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “as she repaid you”.\f* \v 7 As much as she glorified herself and lived in luxury, so much torment and mourning give her; for she says in her heart: I sit as a queen, and I am not a widow, and I will never see mourning.\f + \fr 18:7 \ft “Lived in luxury”: Gr. στρηνιάω (cf. 18:3, 9). “Mourning”: Gr. πένθος. “I sit as a queen, and I am not a widow”: cf. Isa 47:7–9; Zeph 2:15 [LXX 3:1].\f* \v 8 Therefore in one day her plagues will come: pestilence, and mourning, and famine; and she will be burned with fire, for mighty is the Lord God who judged her.\f + \fr 18:8 \ft “Pestilence”: Gr. θάνατος, lit. “death” (cf. 6:8). “In one day”: cf. Isa 47:9. “She will be burned with fire”: cf. 17:16. “Mighty is the Lord God”: cf. Jer 50:34 [LXX 27:34].\f*
\s1 The lament over Babylon
\p \v 9 The kings of the earth, who prostituted themselves and lived in luxury with her, will weep and wail over her when they see the smoke of her burning,\f + \fr 18:9 \ft “Wail”: Gr. κόπτομαι, lit. “beat the breast” (cf. 1:7, “mourn”; Ezek 26:16–17; 27:30–35). “Burning”: Gr. πύρωσις (cf. 18:18).\f* \v 10 standing far off because of the fear of her torment, saying: Woe, woe, the great city, Babylon, the mighty city, because in one hour your judgment has come!\f + \fr 18:10 \ft “Woe, woe”: Gr. οὐαὶ οὐαί (cf. 18:16, 19). “In one hour”: cf. 17:12; 18:17, 19. “Judgment”: Gr. κρίσις (cf. 14:7).\f*
\p \v 11 The merchants of the earth weep and mourn over her, because no one buys their cargo anymore:\f + \fr 18:11 \ft “Cargo”: Gr. γόμος (cf. Ezek 27:27). “Weep and mourn”: presents; the Byzantine text reads “will weep and will mourn”.\f* \v 12 cargo of gold, and of silver, and of precious stones, and of pearls, and of fine linen, and of purple, and of silk, and of scarlet; and all citron wood, and every article of ivory, and every article of most precious wood, and of bronze, and of iron, and of marble;\f + \fr 18:12 \ft “Fine linen”: Gr. βύσσινος (cf. 18:16; 19:8). “Citron wood”: Gr. ξύλον θύϊνον, an aromatic wood. “Most precious”: Gr. τιμιωτάτου. Cf. Ezek 27:12–24.\f* \v 13 and cinnamon, and spice, and incense, and ointment, and frankincense, and wine, and oil, and fine flour, and wheat, and cattle, and sheep; and horses, and carriages, and slaves, and souls of men.\f + \fr 18:13 \ft “Spice”: Gr. ἄμωμον, an aromatic plant; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus omit it. “Ointment”: Gr. μύρον, perfumed oil. “Frankincense”: Gr. λίβανος. “Fine flour”: Gr. σεμίδαλις. “Carriages”: Gr. ῥέδαι, four-wheeled carriages. “Slaves”: Gr. σώματα, lit. “bodies”, a term used for slaves. “Souls of men”: Gr. ψυχὰς ἀνθρώπων (cf. Ezek 27:13).\f* \v 14 And the fruit that your soul longed for has departed from you, and all things fine and splendid are lost to you, and they will never find them again.\f + \fr 18:14 \ft “The fruit that your soul longed for”: Gr. ἡ ὀπώρα σου τῆς ἐπιθυμίας τῆς ψυχῆς, lit. “your fruit of the desire of the soul”. “Fine and splendid”: Gr. λιπαρὰ καὶ λαμπρά. “Never… again”: Gr. οὐκέτι οὐ μή. “They will never find them”: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “you will never find them”.\f*
\p \v 15 The merchants of these things, who grew rich from her, will stand far off because of the fear of her torment, weeping and mourning,\f + \fr 18:15 \ft “Will stand far off”: cf. 18:10, 17.\f* \v 16 saying: Woe, woe, the great city, that was clothed in fine linen, and purple, and scarlet, and adorned with gold, and precious stones, and pearls,\f + \fr 18:16 \ft “Clothed in fine linen, and purple, and scarlet”: cf. 17:4.\f* \v 17 because in one hour such great riches have been made desolate! And every shipmaster, and everyone who sails anywhere, and sailors, and all who work the sea stood far off,\f + \fr 18:17 \ft “Have been made desolate”: Gr. ἠρημώθη (cf. 17:16; 18:19). “Shipmaster”: Gr. κυβερνήτης. “Everyone who sails anywhere”: Gr. ὁ ἐπὶ τόπον πλέων, lit. “the one who sails to a place”; the Textus Receptus reads “all the company in ships”. Cf. Ezek 27:26–36.\f*
\p \v 18 and cried out as they saw the smoke of her burning, saying: Who is like the great city?\f + \fr 18:18 \ft “Cried out”: Gr. ἔκραξαν, aorist; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read ἔκραζον, “were crying out”, also in 18:19. “Who is like”: cf. 13:4; Ezek 27:32.\f* \v 19 They threw dust on their heads, and cried out, weeping and mourning, saying: Woe, woe, the great city, in which all who had ships at sea grew rich from her opulence, because in one hour she has been made desolate!\f + \fr 18:19 \ft “They threw dust on their heads”: cf. Josh 7:6; Ezek 27:30; Lam 2:10. “Opulence”: Gr. τιμιότης.\f* \v 20 Rejoice over her, O heaven, and you saints, and apostles, and prophets, because God has judged your cause against her.\f + \fr 18:20 \ft “Rejoice”: Gr. εὐφραίνου (cf. 12:12). “Saints, and apostles, and prophets”: the Textus Receptus reads “holy apostles”. “God has judged your cause against her”: Gr. ἔκρινεν… τὸ κρίμα ὑμῶν ἐξ αὐτῆς, lit. “has judged your judgment from her” (cf. 6:10; 19:2; Deut 32:43).\f*
\s1 The millstone
\p \v 21 Then a mighty angel took up a stone like a great millstone and cast it into the sea, saying: Thus with force Babylon, the great city, will be cast down, and will never be found again.\f + \fr 18:21 \ft “Mighty angel”: cf. 5:2; 10:1. “Like a great millstone”: Gr. ὡς μύλινον μέγαν. “With force”: Gr. ὁρμήματι. Cf. Jer 51:63–64 [LXX 28:63–64].\f* \v 22 And the voice of harpists, and of musicians, and of flute players, and of trumpeters will never be heard in you again; and no craftsman of any craft will be found in you again; and the sound of the millstone will never be heard in you again;\f + \fr 18:22 \ft “Harpists”: Gr. κιθαρῳδοί (cf. 14:2). “No craftsman of any craft”: Gr. πᾶς τεχνίτης πάσης τέχνης οὐ μή, lit. “every craftsman of every craft… not”. Cf. Isa 24:8; Jer 25:10; Ezek 26:13.\f* \v 23 and the light of a lamp will never shine in you again; and the voice of the bridegroom and of the bride will never be heard in you again; because your merchants were the great ones of the earth, because by your sorcery all the nations were deceived.\f + \fr 18:23 \ft “The voice of the bridegroom and of the bride”: cf. Jer 7:34; 16:9; 25:10. “The great ones of the earth”: Gr. μεγιστᾶνες (cf. 6:15). “Sorcery”: Gr. φαρμακεία (cf. 9:21; Isa 47:9, 12). “Deceived”: Gr. πλανάω (cf. 12:9; 13:14).\f* \v 24 And in her was found the blood of prophets, and of saints, and of all who have been slain on the earth.\f + \fr 18:24 \ft “Blood of prophets, and of saints”: cf. 16:6; 17:6; 19:2; Matt 23:35. “Slain”: Gr. σφάζω (cf. 6:9).\f*
\c 19
\s1 Hallelujah!
\p \v 1 After these things I heard as it were a loud voice of a great multitude in heaven, saying:
\q1 Hallelujah!
\q1 Salvation, and glory, and power belong to our God,\f + \fr 19:1 \ft “As it were a loud voice of a great multitude”: cf. 7:9–10; 19:6. “Hallelujah”: Gr. ἁλληλουϊά, from the Hebrew, “praise Yah” (cf. Ps 104:35 [LXX 103:35]; 106:1, 48 [LXX 105:1, 48]). “Belong to our God”: the Greek has no verb (cf. 7:10; also 12:10). Variant: the Textus Receptus adds “and the honor” and reads “to the Lord our God”.\f*
\q1 \v 2 because true and just are His judgments;
\q1 because He has judged the great prostitute,
\q1 who corrupted the earth with her prostitution,
\q1 and He has avenged the blood of His servants at her hand.\f + \fr 19:2 \ft “True and just are His judgments”: cf. 15:3; 16:7. “Corrupted”: Gr. ἔφθειρεν (cf. 11:18, διαφθείρω); the Byzantine text reads διέφθειρεν. “Has avenged the blood of His servants”: cf. 6:10; Deut 32:43; 2 Kgs 9:7. “At her hand”: that is, exacting it from her (cf. 2 Kgs 9:7; Rev 18:20).\f*
\p \v 3 And they said a second time:
\q1 Hallelujah!
\q1 And her smoke goes up forever and ever.\f + \fr 19:3 \ft “A second time”: Gr. δεύτερον. “Said”: Gr. εἴρηκαν, perfect. “Her smoke goes up”: cf. 14:11; 18:9, 18; Isa 34:10.\f*
\p \v 4 And the twenty-four elders and the four living creatures fell down and worshiped God, who is seated on the throne, saying:
\q1 Amen! Hallelujah!\f + \fr 19:4 \ft “Fell down and worshiped”: cf. 4:10; 5:14; 11:16. “Amen! Hallelujah!”: cf. Ps 106:48 [LXX 105:48].\f*
\p \v 5 And a voice came out from the throne, saying:
\q1 Praise our God, all His servants,
\q1 and you who fear Him, the small and the great.\f + \fr 19:5 \ft “Praise our God”: Gr. αἰνεῖτε τῷ θεῷ ἡμῶν (cf. Ps 134:1 [LXX 133:1]; 135:1, 20 [LXX 134:1, 20]). “The small and the great”: cf. 11:18; 13:16; Ps 115:13 [LXX 113:21].\f*
\p \v 6 And I heard as it were the voice of a great multitude, and as it were the voice of many waters, and as it were the voice of mighty thunders, saying:
\q1 Hallelujah!
\q1 For the Lord God, the Almighty, has begun to reign.\f + \fr 19:6 \ft “Voice of many waters… of mighty thunders”: cf. 1:15; 14:2. “Has begun to reign”: Gr. ἐβασίλευσεν, aorist (cf. 11:17; Ps 93:1 [LXX 92:1]; 97:1 [LXX 96:1]; 99:1 [LXX 98:1]). “The Lord God”: the Byzantine text reads “the Lord our God”.\f*
\q1 \v 7 Let us rejoice and exult,
\q1 and we will give Him the glory,
\q1 because the wedding of the Lamb has come,
\q1 and His wife has made herself ready;\f + \fr 19:7 \ft “Let us rejoice and exult”: cf. Ps 118:24 [LXX 117:24]; Matt 5:12. “And we will give”: Gr. δώσομεν, future; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “let us give”. “Wedding”: Gr. γάμος (cf. 19:9; Matt 22:2–14). “His wife”: Gr. ἡ γυνὴ αὐτοῦ (cf. 21:2, 9; Isa 54:5–6; Hos 2:19–20 (Heb. 2:21–22)).\f*
\p \v 8 and it was granted to her to clothe herself in fine linen, bright and pure; for the fine linen is the righteous acts of the saints.\f + \fr 19:8 \ft “Clothe herself”: Gr. περιβάλλω. “Fine linen”: Gr. βύσσινος (cf. 18:12, 16; 19:14; also 15:6, Gr. λίνον). “Righteous acts”: Gr. δικαιώματα (cf. 15:4). Cf. Isa 61:10.\f*
\p \v 9 Then he says to me: Write: Blessed are those who have been called to the wedding supper of the Lamb. And he says to me: These words are true words of God.\f + \fr 19:9 \ft “Write”: cf. 14:13. “Blessed”: cf. 1:3; 14:13; 16:15. “Wedding supper”: cf. Matt 22:2–10; Luke 14:15–24. “Have been called”: Gr. κεκλημένοι, perfect. “These words are true words of God”: lit. “these words true of God are” (cf. 21:5; 22:6).\f* \v 10 And I fell down before his feet to worship him. And he says to me: See that you do not do that; I am a fellow servant of yours and of your brothers who have the Testimony of Jesus. Worship God; for the Testimony of Jesus is the Spirit of prophecy.\f + \fr 19:10 \ft “To worship him”: cf. 22:8–9. “See that you do not do that”: Gr. ὅρα μή, lit. “see, not”. “Fellow servant”: Gr. σύνδουλος (cf. 6:11; 22:9). “The Testimony of Jesus”: cf. 1:2, 9; 12:17. “The Spirit of prophecy”: Gr. τὸ πνεῦμα τῆς προφητείας; the capital follows the usage of the book, in which “the Spirit” used absolutely is the Holy Spirit (cf. 2:7; 14:13; 22:17; 1 Pet 1:10–11; 2 Pet 1:21); alternative reading: “the spirit”, that is, the essence of prophecy.\f*
\s1 The Rider on the white horse
\p \v 11 And I saw heaven opened, and behold, a white horse, and He who sat on it is called Faithful and True, and in righteousness He judges and makes war.\f + \fr 19:11 \ft “He”: the capital follows the identification given by the text itself: “Faithful and True” (3:14), “the Word of God” (19:13), “King of kings and Lord of lords” (19:16; cf. 17:14). “Is called”: Gr. καλούμενος, participle. “Heaven opened”: cf. 4:1; Ezek 1:1. “White horse”: cf. 6:2. “Faithful and True”: cf. 1:5; 3:7, 14. “In righteousness He judges”: cf. Ps 96:13 [LXX 95:13]; Isa 11:4.\f* \v 12 His eyes are a flame of fire, and on His head are many diadems, and He has a name written that no one knows except He Himself;\f + \fr 19:12 \ft “Eyes… a flame of fire”: cf. 1:14; 2:18; Dan 10:6; the Textus Receptus adds “as”. “Diadems”: Gr. διαδήματα (cf. 12:3; 13:1). “A name written that no one knows”: cf. 2:17; 3:12. Variant: the Byzantine text adds “names written, and”.\f* \v 13 and He is clothed in a garment dipped in blood; and His name is called: the Word of God.\f + \fr 19:13 \ft “Clothed”: Gr. περιβάλλω. “Dipped in blood”: Gr. βεβαμμένον αἵματι (cf. Isa 63:1–3); some manuscripts read “sprinkled with blood”. “The Word of God”: Gr. ὁ Λόγος τοῦ Θεοῦ (cf. John 1:1, 14; Rev 1:2, 9). “Is called”: Gr. κέκληται, perfect; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read καλεῖται.\f* \v 14 The armies that are in heaven followed Him on white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and pure.\f + \fr 19:14 \ft “Armies”: Gr. στρατεύματα. “Fine linen, white and pure”: cf. 19:8.\f* \v 15 From His mouth comes a sharp sword, so that with it He may strike the nations; and He will shepherd them with a rod of iron; and He treads the winepress of the wine of the fury of the wrath of God, the Almighty.\f + \fr 19:15 \ft “Sharp sword”: Gr. ῥομφαία ὀξεῖα (cf. 1:16; 2:12, 16; Isa 11:4; 49:2); the Byzantine text adds “two-edged”. “Strike”: Gr. πατάσσω (cf. 11:6). “Shepherd them with a rod of iron”: cf. 2:27; 12:5; Ps 2:9. “Treads the winepress”: cf. 14:19–20; Isa 63:3; Joel 3:13 (Heb. 4:13); the Textus Receptus reads “of the fury and of the wrath”.\f* \v 16 He has on His garment and on His thigh a name written: King of kings and Lord of lords.\f + \fr 19:16 \ft “Thigh”: Gr. μηρός. “King of kings and Lord of lords”: cf. 17:14; Deut 10:17; 1 Tim 6:15.\f*
\s1 The great supper of God
\p \v 17 And I saw an angel standing in the sun, and he cried out with a loud voice, saying to all the birds that fly in midheaven: Come, gather together for the great supper of God,\f + \fr 19:17 \ft “Birds that fly in midheaven”: cf. 8:13; 14:6. “Come, gather together”: Gr. δεῦτε συνάχθητε. “The great supper of God”: cf. Ezek 39:17–20; the Textus Receptus reads “the supper of the great God”.\f* \v 18 to eat the flesh of kings, and the flesh of commanders, and the flesh of mighty men, and the flesh of horses and of those who sit on them, and the flesh of all, free and slaves, and small and great.\f + \fr 19:18 \ft “Commanders”: Gr. χιλίαρχοι (cf. 6:15). “Free and slaves, and small and great”: cf. 6:15; 13:16.\f*
\p \v 19 And I saw the beast, and the kings of the earth, and their armies, gathered to make the war against Him who sat on the horse and against His army.\f + \fr 19:19 \ft “Gathered to make the war”: Gr. τὸν πόλεμον, with the article (cf. 16:14, 16; 17:14; 20:8; Ps 2:2); the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus omit the article.\f* \v 20 Then the beast was seized, and with it the false prophet, who had performed before it the signs by which he deceived those who received the mark of the beast and those who worshiped its image; the two were cast alive into the lake of fire that burns with sulfur.\f + \fr 19:20 \ft “Was seized”: Gr. ἐπιάσθη. “The false prophet”: cf. 13:11–17; 16:13. “Lake of fire that burns with sulfur”: cf. 14:10; 20:10, 14–15; 21:8; Dan 7:11.\f* \v 21 The rest were killed with the sword that came out of the mouth of Him who sat on the horse; and all the birds were filled with their flesh.\f + \fr 19:21 \ft “The rest”: Gr. οἱ λοιποί (cf. 9:20). “Came out”: Gr. ἐξελθούσῃ, aorist; the Textus Receptus reads “comes out”. “Were filled”: cf. Ezek 39:4, 17–20.\f*
\c 20
\s1 The thousand years
\p \v 1 And I saw an angel coming down from heaven, having the key of the abyss and a great chain in his hand.\f + \fr 20:1 \ft “The key of the abyss”: cf. 1:18; 9:1. “Abyss”: Gr. ἄβυσσος, the same word as in the LXX of Gen 1:2. Cf. Jer 4:23–26. “Chain”: Gr. ἅλυσις.\f* \v 2 He seized the dragon, the ancient serpent, who is the devil and Satan, and bound him for a thousand years,\f + \fr 20:2 \ft “Seized”: Gr. ἐκράτησεν. “The ancient serpent, who is the devil and Satan”: cf. 12:9; Gen 3:1. “A thousand years”: Gr. χίλια ἔτη (cf. 20:3–7). “Bound him”: cf. Isa 24:21–22; Matt 12:29. Variant: the Byzantine text adds “who deceives the whole world”.\f* \v 3 and cast him into the abyss, and shut and sealed it over him, so that he would no longer deceive the nations, until the thousand years were completed; after these things he must be released for a little while.\f + \fr 20:3 \ft “Shut and sealed it over him”: Gr. ἔκλεισεν καὶ ἐσφράγισεν ἐπάνω αὐτοῦ, that is, the abyss (cf. Dan 6:17 (Heb. 6:18); Matt 27:66); the Textus Receptus reads “shut him”. “Deceive”: Gr. πλανάω (cf. 12:9; 20:8, 10). “Were completed”: Gr. τελεσθῇ (cf. 17:17; 20:5, 7). “A little while”: Gr. μικρὸν χρόνον (cf. 6:11).\f*
\p \v 4 And I saw thrones, and they sat on them, and judgment was given to them; and I saw the souls of those who had been beheaded because of the Testimony of Jesus and because of the Word of God, and those who had not worshiped the beast or its image, and had not received the mark on their forehead and on their hand; and they came to life and reigned with Christ for a thousand years.\f + \fr 20:4 \ft “Thrones… judgment”: Gr. κρίμα (cf. Dan 7:9, 22, 26; Matt 19:28; 1 Cor 6:2–3). “And I saw the souls”: “I saw” is implied in the Greek. “The souls”: Gr. ψυχαί (cf. 6:9). “Beheaded”: Gr. πεπελεκισμένων, lit. “beheaded with an axe”. “The Testimony of Jesus… the Word of God”: cf. 1:2, 9; 6:9; 12:17. “Mark”: cf. 13:16–17; 14:9. “Came to life”: Gr. ἔζησαν, lit. “lived”, an ingressive aorist, “began to live” (cf. 2:8; 13:14; Ezek 37:10). “Christ”: Gr. τοῦ χριστοῦ, with the article, “the Christ”; the Textus Receptus omits the article.\f* \v 5 The rest of the dead did not come to life until the thousand years were completed. This is the first resurrection.\f + \fr 20:5 \ft “The rest of the dead”: Gr. οἱ λοιποὶ τῶν νεκρῶν. “Did not come to life”: lit. “did not live” (cf. 20:4); the Textus Receptus reads ἀνέζησαν. Variant: Codex Sinaiticus and part of the Byzantine tradition omit the first sentence of the verse. “The first resurrection”: cf. John 5:28–29; 1 Thess 4:16; Luke 14:14.\f* \v 6 Blessed and holy is the one who has part in the first resurrection; over these the second death has no authority, but they will be priests of God and of Christ, and will reign with Him for a thousand years.\f + \fr 20:6 \ft “Blessed”: cf. 1:3; 14:13; 19:9. “The second death”: cf. 2:11; 20:14; 21:8. “Priests of God and of Christ”: cf. 1:6; 5:10; Ex 19:6. “Will reign with Him for a thousand years”: cf. 5:10; 22:5.\f*
\s1 The defeat of Satan
\p \v 7 When the thousand years are completed, Satan will be released from his prison,\f + \fr 20:7 \ft “Prison”: Gr. φυλακή (cf. 2:10; 18:2).\f* \v 8 and will go out to deceive the nations that are at the four corners of the earth, Gog and Magog, to gather them for the war; their number is like the sand of the sea.\f + \fr 20:8 \ft “The four corners of the earth”: cf. 7:1. “Gog and Magog”: cf. Ezek 38–39. “For the war”: Gr. τὸν πόλεμον (cf. 16:14; 19:19); the Textus Receptus omits the article. “Like the sand of the sea”: cf. Gen 22:17; Josh 11:4.\f* \v 9 They went up over the breadth of the earth, and surrounded the camp of the saints and the beloved city; and fire came down from heaven and devoured them.\f + \fr 20:9 \ft “The breadth of the earth”: Gr. τὸ πλάτος τῆς γῆς (cf. Hab 1:6). “Over the breadth of the earth”: lit. “upon the breadth of the earth”. “Camp”: Gr. παρεμβολή. “The beloved city”: cf. Ps 87:2 [LXX 86:2]; Jer 11:15. “Fire came down from heaven”: cf. 2 Kgs 1:10–12; Ezek 38:22; 39:6; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus add “from God”.\f* \v 10 The devil, who deceived them, was cast into the lake of fire and sulfur, where the beast and the false prophet also are; and they will be tormented day and night forever and ever.\f + \fr 20:10 \ft “The lake of fire and sulfur”: cf. 19:20; 21:8. “Will be tormented”: Gr. βασανίζω (cf. 14:10–11). “Forever and ever”: cf. 14:11; Isa 34:9–10; Jude 7.\f*
\s1 The judgment before the great white throne
\p \v 11 And I saw a great white throne and Him who sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away, and no place was found for them.\f + \fr 20:11 \ft “Great white throne”: cf. Dan 7:9–10. “From whose face the earth and the heaven fled away”: cf. 6:14; 16:20; 21:1; Ps 102:25–26 (Heb. 102:26–27) [LXX 101:26–27]; 2 Pet 3:10.\f* \v 12 And I saw the dead, the great and the small, standing before the throne; and books were opened; and another book was opened, which is the book of life; and the dead were judged by the things that were written in the books, according to their works.\f + \fr 20:12 \ft “The great and the small”: cf. 11:18; 19:5. “Books were opened”: cf. Dan 7:10; 12:1; Mal 3:16. “Which is the book of life”: lit. “which is of life” (cf. 3:5; 13:8; 17:8; 21:27). “According to their works”: cf. 2:23; 22:12; Ps 62:12 (Heb. 62:13) [LXX 61:13]; Rom 2:6. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “before God” instead of “before the throne”, and “small and great”.\f* \v 13 The sea gave up the dead who were in it, and death and the grave gave up the dead who were in them; and they were judged, each one according to their works.\f + \fr 20:13 \ft “Death and the grave”: Gr. ὁ θάνατος καὶ ὁ ᾅδης (cf. 1:18; 6:8). “Each one”: Gr. ἕκαστος.\f* \v 14 Then death and the grave were cast into the lake of fire. This is the second death, the lake of fire.\f + \fr 20:14 \ft “The second death, the lake of fire”: cf. 2:11; 20:6; 21:8; Isa 25:8; 1 Cor 15:26; the Textus Receptus omits “the lake of fire”.\f* \v 15 If anyone was not found written in the book of life, he was cast into the lake of fire.\f + \fr 20:15 \ft “Book of life”: Gr. ἡ βίβλος τῆς ζωῆς (cf. 3:5; 13:8; Dan 12:1).\f*
\c 21
\s1 The new heaven and the new earth
\p \v 1 And I saw a new heaven and a new earth; for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away, and the sea is no more.\f + \fr 21:1 \ft “A new heaven and a new earth”: Gr. καινός, “new” in quality (cf. 2:17; 3:12; 5:9; Isa 65:17; 66:22; 2 Pet 3:13). “Had passed away”: Gr. ἀπῆλθαν, lit. “went away” (cf. 20:11; 21:4); the Textus Receptus reads παρῆλθε. “The sea is no more”: cf. 13:1; 20:13. Variant: Codex Alexandrinus reads “and the sea I saw no more”.\f* \v 2 And I saw the holy city, the new Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband.\f + \fr 21:2 \ft “The holy city, the new Jerusalem”: cf. 3:12; 11:2; Isa 52:1; Heb 11:10, 16; 12:22. “From God”: Gr. ἀπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ (cf. 3:12; 21:10). “Bride”: Gr. νύμφη (cf. 18:23; 21:9; 22:17). “Adorned”: Gr. κοσμέω (cf. 21:19; Isa 61:10). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “And I, John, saw the holy city”.\f* \v 3 And I heard a loud voice from the throne, saying:
\q1 Behold, the tabernacle of God is with men,
\q1 and He will have His tabernacle with them,
\q1 and they will be His peoples,
\q1 and God Himself will be with them,\f + \fr 21:3 \ft “From the throne”: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “from heaven”. “Tabernacle… have His tabernacle”: Gr. σκηνή… σκηνώσει (cf. 7:15; 12:12; 13:6; John 1:14); an echo of Lev 26:11–12 and Ezek 37:27. “His peoples”: Gr. λαοί, plural (cf. Zech 2:11 (Heb. 2:15)); the Byzantine text reads the singular, “His people”. Variant: Codex Alexandrinus and the Textus Receptus add “their God” at the end of the verse.\f*
\q1 \v 4 and He will wipe away every tear from their eyes,
\q1 and death will be no more,
\q1 nor will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain anymore;
\q1 the first things have passed away.\f + \fr 21:4 \ft “Wipe away every tear”: cf. 7:17; Isa 25:8. “Death will be no more”: cf. 20:14; 1 Cor 15:26, 54. “Mourning”: Gr. πένθος (cf. 18:7–8). “Crying”: Gr. κραυγή, lit. “outcry, shout”. “Pain”: Gr. πόνος (cf. 16:10–11). “The first things have passed away”: cf. Isa 43:18–19; 65:16–17; 2 Cor 5:17. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “for the first things have passed away”; the Textus Receptus adds “God” after “will wipe away”.\f*
\p \v 5 And He who is seated on the throne said: Behold, I make all things new. And He says: Write, for these words are faithful and true.\f + \fr 21:5 \ft “Behold, I make all things new”: Gr. καινὰ ποιῶ πάντα (cf. Isa 43:19). “And He says”: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus add “to me”. “Faithful and true”: cf. 3:14; 19:9, 11; 22:6; the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “true and faithful”.\f* \v 6 And He said to me: It is done! I am the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End. To the one who is thirsty I will give freely from the spring of the water of life.\f + \fr 21:6 \ft “It is done”: Gr. γέγοναν, perfect plural, lit. “They are done”, referring to “all things” in v. 5 (cf. 16:17). Variants: Codex Sinaiticus and the Byzantine text read γέγονα; the Textus Receptus reads the singular γέγονε. “The Alpha and the Omega”: cf. 1:8; 22:13. “The Beginning and the End”: Gr. ἡ ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ τέλος (cf. 3:14; 22:13; Isa 44:6). “To the one who is thirsty… freely”: Gr. δωρεάν (cf. 7:16–17; 22:17; Isa 55:1; John 4:10, 14).\f* \v 7 The one who conquers will inherit these things, and I will be his God, and he will be My son.\f + \fr 21:7 \ft “The one who conquers”: cf. 2:7, 11, 17, 26; 3:5, 12, 21. “Will inherit these things”: the Textus Receptus reads “will inherit all things”. “I will be his God, and he will be My son”: lit. “I will be God to him, and he will be son to Me” (cf. 2 Sam 7:14; Ps 89:26–27 (Heb. 89:27–28) [LXX 88:27–28]; Jer 31:33 [LXX 38:33]).\f* \v 8 But as for the cowardly, and unfaithful, and abominable, and murderers, and the immoral, and sorcerers, and idolaters, and all liars, their part will be in the lake that burns with fire and sulfur, which is the second death.\f + \fr 21:8 \ft “Unfaithful”: Gr. ἄπιστοι, in contrast with the “faithful” (πιστοί) words of v. 5; or “unbelieving”. “Abominable”: Gr. ἐβδελυγμένοι, “those who have become abominable” (cf. 17:4–5; 21:27). “Murderers”: Gr. φονεῖς (cf. 9:21). “The immoral”: Gr. πόρνοι, those who practice prostitution, πορνεία (cf. 2:14, 20–21; 22:15). “Sorcerers”: Gr. φάρμακοι (cf. 9:21; 18:23; 22:15). “The second death”: cf. 2:11; 20:6, 14. Variant: the Byzantine text adds “and sinners” after “unfaithful”.\f*
\s1 The new Jerusalem
\p \v 9 And one of the seven angels who had the seven bowls full of the seven last plagues came and spoke with me, saying: Come, I will show you the bride, the wife of the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:9 \ft “One of the seven angels who had the seven bowls”: cf. 15:1, 7; 17:1. “Full”: Gr. γεμόντων, which in the Greek agrees with “angels”; rendered by the sense, as in the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus, which make it agree with “bowls” (γεμούσας). “The bride, the wife of the Lamb”: cf. 19:7–8; 21:2. The phrase mirrors 17:1: the bride-city in contrast with the prostitute-city.\f* \v 10 And he carried me away in the Spirit to a great and high mountain, and showed me the holy city, Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God,\f + \fr 21:10 \ft “Carried me away in the Spirit”: Gr. ἀπήνεγκέν με ἐν πνεύματι, as in 17:3 (cf. 1:10; 4:2). “A great and high mountain”: cf. Ezek 40:2; Isa 2:2. “The holy city, Jerusalem”: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “the great city, the holy Jerusalem”.\f* \v 11 having the glory of God; its radiance was like a most precious stone, like a jasper stone, clear as crystal;\f + \fr 21:11 \ft “The glory of God”: cf. 15:8; 21:23; Isa 60:1–2; Ezek 43:2–5. “Radiance”: Gr. φωστήρ, “luminary, brilliance”. “Jasper”: cf. 4:3; 21:18–19. “Clear as crystal”: Gr. κρυσταλλίζω, “to shine like crystal” (cf. 4:6; 22:1).\f* \v 12 having a great and high wall, having twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names inscribed, which are those of the twelve tribes of the sons of Israel:\f + \fr 21:12 \ft “Gates”: Gr. πυλών, the gate with its gateway (cf. 22:14). “Names inscribed… of the twelve tribes of the sons of Israel”: cf. 7:4–8; Ezek 48:30–34; Ex 28:21.\f* \v 13 on the east, three gates; and on the north, three gates; and on the south, three gates; and on the west, three gates.\f + \fr 21:13 \ft “On the east… on the west”: lit. “from the rising… from the setting” (cf. Ezek 48:31–34; Luke 13:29).\f* \v 14 The wall of the city had twelve foundations, and on them twelve names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:14 \ft “Foundations”: Gr. θεμέλιοι (cf. Heb 11:10; Eph 2:20). “Twelve names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb”: cf. Matt 10:2; the Textus Receptus reads “and in them the names”.\f*
\p \v 15 The one who spoke with me had as a measure a golden reed, to measure the city, and its gates, and its wall.\f + \fr 21:15 \ft “Reed”: Gr. κάλαμος, a measuring reed (cf. 11:1; Ezek 40:3, 5; Zech 2:1–2 (Heb. 2:5–6)). “As a measure”: Gr. μέτρον; the Textus Receptus omits it.\f* \v 16 The city lies foursquare, and its length is as great as its width. And he measured the city with the reed: twelve thousand stadia; its length, and width, and height are equal.\f + \fr 21:16 \ft “Lies foursquare”: Gr. τετράγωνος κεῖται (cf. Ezek 43:16; 48:16–20); equal in length, width and height, like the Most Holy Place (1 Kgs 6:20). “Twelve thousand stadia”: about 2,220 kilometers (1,380 miles); one stadion, about 185 meters (607 feet) (cf. 14:20). The number 12 × 1,000 takes up 7:4–8.\f* \v 17 And he measured its wall: one hundred and forty-four cubits, by a man’s measure, which is the angel’s.\f + \fr 21:17 \ft “One hundred and forty-four cubits”: about 65 meters (216 feet); one cubit, about 45 centimeters (18 inches). The number 12 × 12 takes up 7:4; 14:1. It is not said whether the measure is the height or the thickness of the wall. “By a man’s measure, which is the angel’s”: that is, the angel used the ordinary human measure.\f* \v 18 The material of its wall was jasper, and the city was pure gold, like pure glass.\f + \fr 21:18 \ft “Material”: Gr. ἐνδώμησις, “structure, building material”. “Pure gold, like pure glass”: Gr. καθαρός in both expressions (cf. 21:21).\f* \v 19 The foundations of the wall of the city were adorned with every kind of precious stone: the first foundation, jasper; the second, sapphire; the third, chalcedony; the fourth, emerald;\f + \fr 21:19 \ft “Adorned”: Gr. κοσμέω (cf. 21:2). “Every kind of precious stone”: Gr. παντὶ λίθῳ τιμίῳ, lit. “every precious stone”; cf. Ex 28:17–20; Isa 54:11–12; Ezek 28:13. The twelve stones correspond, broadly, to those of the high priest’s breastpiece. “Sapphire”: Gr. σάπφιρος, probably lapis lazuli.\f* \v 20 the fifth, sardonyx; the sixth, sardius; the seventh, chrysolite; the eighth, beryl; the ninth, topaz; the tenth, chrysoprase; the eleventh, jacinth; the twelfth, amethyst.\f + \fr 21:20 \ft The exact identification of some stones is uncertain. “Sardonyx”: Gr. σαρδόνυξ. “Sardius”: Gr. σάρδιον (cf. 4:3). “Chrysolite”: Gr. χρυσόλιθος, lit. “gold stone”. “Chrysoprase”: Gr. χρυσόπρασος, a golden-green stone. “Jacinth”: Gr. ὑάκινθος (cf. 9:17).\f* \v 21 The twelve gates were twelve pearls; each of the gates was of a single pearl. The street of the city was pure gold, like transparent glass.\f + \fr 21:21 \ft “Pearls”: Gr. μαργαρῖται (cf. 17:4; 18:12, 16; Matt 13:45–46). “Street”: Gr. πλατεῖα, the main street (cf. 11:8; 22:2). “Transparent glass”: Gr. ὕαλος διαυγής; the Textus Receptus reads διαφανής.\f*
\p \v 22 And I saw no sanctuary in it, for the Lord God, the Almighty, is its sanctuary, and the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:22 \ft “Sanctuary”: Gr. ναός (cf. 3:12; 7:15; 11:19). The sanctuary of the city is the presence of God and of the Lamb (cf. 21:3; 22:3–4; John 2:19–21), and the city itself has the cubic form of the Most Holy Place (cf. 21:16; 1 Kgs 6:20). Alternative reading: the verse stresses the primacy of the divine presence, before which any structure becomes secondary. “The Lord God, the Almighty”: cf. 1:8; 4:8; 11:17; 15:3.\f* \v 23 The city has no need of the sun, nor of the moon, to shine on it, for the glory of God has illuminated it, and its lamp is the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:23 \ft “No need of the sun, nor of the moon”: cf. 22:5; Isa 60:19–20. “Has illuminated it”: Gr. ἐφώτισεν, aorist. “Its lamp is the Lamb”: Gr. λύχνος (cf. 18:23; 22:5; John 8:12).\f* \v 24 The nations will walk by its light; and the kings of the earth bring their glory into it.\f + \fr 21:24 \ft “The nations will walk by its light”: Gr. διὰ τοῦ φωτὸς αὐτῆς, lit. “through its light” (cf. Isa 60:3, 5, 11). “Their glory”: Gr. αὐτῶν, the glory of the kings. “Bring”: present in the Greek. Variants: the Byzantine text reads “bring to it glory and honor of the nations”; the Textus Receptus reads “the nations of those who are saved will walk in its light” and “their glory and honor”.\f* \v 25 Its gates will never be shut by day, for there will be no night there;\f + \fr 21:25 \ft “Will never be shut by day”: Gr. οὐ μὴ κλεισθῶσιν (cf. Isa 60:11; Zech 14:7). “There will be no night”: cf. 22:5.\f* \v 26 and they will bring into it the glory and the honor of the nations.\f + \fr 21:26 \ft “The glory and the honor of the nations”: cf. Isa 60:5, 11; 66:12.\f* \v 27 And nothing profane will ever enter it, nor anyone who practices abomination and falsehood, but only those who are written in the book of life of the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:27 \ft “Nothing profane”: Gr. πᾶν κοινόν, “everything common, profane”; the Textus Receptus reads κοινοῦν, “that which defiles”. “Abomination and falsehood”: cf. 21:8; 22:15. “Written in the book of life of the Lamb”: cf. 3:5; 13:8; 17:8; 20:12, 15; Isa 52:1; Ezek 44:9.\f*
\c 22
\s1 The river of the water of life
\p \v 1 And he showed me a river of the water of life, bright as crystal, coming out from the throne of God and of the Lamb,\f + \fr 22:1 \ft “River of the water of life”: cf. 7:17; 21:6; Ezek 47:1–12; Zech 14:8; Ps 46:4 (Heb. 46:5) [LXX 45:5]; John 7:38. “Bright”: Gr. λαμπρός (cf. 15:6; 19:8). “Coming out”: Gr. ἐκπορεύομαι. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “a pure river of the water of life”.\f* \v 2 in the middle of its street. On either side of the river is the tree of life, producing twelve fruits, yielding its fruit every month; and the leaves of the tree are for the healing of the nations.\f + \fr 22:2 \ft “Street”: Gr. πλατεῖα (cf. 21:21). “On either side of the river”: Gr. ἐντεῦθεν καὶ ἐκεῖθεν (cf. Ezek 47:7, 12). “The tree of life”: Gr. ξύλον ζωῆς, possibly collective, “trees” (cf. 2:7; 22:14, 19; Gen 2:9; 3:22–24). “Every month”: lit. “according to each month”. “Healing”: Gr. θεραπεία (cf. Ezek 47:12).\f* \v 3 There will no longer be any curse. And the throne of God and of the Lamb will be in it, and His servants will serve Him,\f + \fr 22:3 \ft “Curse”: Gr. κατάθεμα, “accursed thing” (cf. Gen 3:17; Zech 14:11); the Textus Receptus reads κατανάθεμα. “The throne of God and of the Lamb”: cf. 3:21; 7:17. “His servants will serve Him”: Gr. αὐτοῦ… αὐτῷ, singular, referring to God and the Lamb together (cf. 11:15; 20:6). “Serve”: Gr. λατρεύω, to serve in worship (cf. 7:15).\f* \v 4 and they will see His face, and His name will be on their foreheads.\f + \fr 22:4 \ft “They will see His face”: cf. Ex 33:20, 23; Ps 17:15 [LXX 16:15]; Matt 5:8; 1 Cor 13:12; 1 John 3:2. “His name… on their foreheads”: cf. 3:12; 7:3; 14:1, in contrast with 13:16–17.\f* \v 5 There will be no more night, and they have no need of the light of a lamp or the light of the sun, for the Lord God will shine on them; and they will reign forever and ever.\f + \fr 22:5 \ft “There will be no more night”: cf. 21:25; Zech 14:7. “Have no need”: present in the Greek (cf. 21:23). “Will shine on them”: Gr. φωτίσει ἐπ’ αὐτούς, lit. “will give light upon them” (cf. Isa 60:19–20; Num 6:25). “Will reign”: cf. 5:10; 20:4, 6; Dan 7:18, 27. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “there will be no night there” and “need of a lamp”; the Textus Receptus reads “gives them light”.\f*
\s1 Behold, I am coming soon
\p \v 6 And he said to me: These words are faithful and true; and the Lord, the God of the spirits of the prophets, has sent His angel to show His servants the things that must soon happen.\f + \fr 22:6 \ft “Faithful and true”: cf. 19:9; 21:5. “The God of the spirits of the prophets”: cf. Num 27:16; 1 Cor 14:32; the Textus Receptus reads “the God of the holy prophets”; the Byzantine text reads “And he says to me”. “To show His servants the things that must soon happen”: takes up 1:1 and frames the book.\f* \v 7 And behold, I am coming soon. Blessed is the one who keeps the words of the prophecy of this book.\f + \fr 22:7 \ft “Behold, I am coming soon”: cf. 2:16; 3:11; 22:12, 20. “Blessed”: cf. 1:3; 14:13; 16:15; 19:9; 20:6; 22:14, the sixth of the seven beatitudes of the book. “Keeps”: Gr. τηρέω (cf. 1:3; 3:10; 12:17; 14:12).\f*
\p \v 8 And I, John, am the one who heard and saw these things. When I heard and saw, I fell down to worship before the feet of the angel who showed me these things.\f + \fr 22:8 \ft “Am the one who heard and saw”: Gr. ὁ ἀκούων καὶ βλέπων, present participles, lit. “the one who hears and sees” (cf. 1:1, 4, 9). “I fell down to worship”: cf. 19:10.\f* \v 9 And he says to me: See that you do not do that; I am a fellow servant of yours and of your brothers the prophets, and of those who keep the words of this book. Worship God.\f + \fr 22:9 \ft “See that you do not do that”: Gr. ὅρα μή (cf. 19:10). “Fellow servant”: Gr. σύνδουλος (cf. 6:11; 19:10). “The prophets, and of those who keep the words of this book”: cf. 19:10. “Worship God”: cf. 14:7.\f*
\p \v 10 And he says to me: Do not seal up the words of the prophecy of this book, for the time is near.\f + \fr 22:10 \ft “Do not seal up”: in contrast with Dan 8:26; 12:4, 9 (cf. 10:4). “The time is near”: Gr. ὁ καιρὸς ἐγγύς (cf. 1:3).\f* \v 11 Let the one who does wrong still do wrong; and the filthy still be filthy; and the righteous still do righteousness; and the holy still be made holy.\f + \fr 22:11 \ft “The one who does wrong”: Gr. ὁ ἀδικῶν, “the one who practices injustice”; the same verb is “harm” in 2:11; 7:2–3. “Still”: Gr. ἔτι, “let him continue”. Cf. Dan 12:10; Ezek 3:27. “Be made holy”: Gr. ἁγιασθήτω (cf. Lev 11:44). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “and the righteous, let him be made righteous still”.\f* \v 12 Behold, I am coming soon, and My reward is with Me, to repay each one according to his work.\f + \fr 22:12 \ft “Reward”: Gr. μισθός (cf. 11:18; Isa 40:10; 62:11). “To repay each one according to his work”: lit. “as his work is” (cf. 2:23; 20:12–13; Ps 62:12 (Heb. 62:13) [LXX 61:13]; Prov 24:12; Matt 16:27; Rom 2:6). Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “as his work will be”.\f* \v 13 I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End.\f + \fr 22:13 \ft “The Alpha and the Omega”: cf. 1:8; 21:6. “The First and the Last”: cf. 1:17; 2:8; Isa 44:6; 48:12. “The Beginning and the End”: cf. 3:14; 21:6. Titles of God in 1:8 and 21:6, here on the lips of Jesus (cf. 22:16). Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “the Beginning and the End, the First and the Last”.\f* \v 14 Blessed are those who wash their robes, so that they may have the right to the tree of life and may enter the city by the gates.\f + \fr 22:14 \ft “Blessed”: the seventh of the seven beatitudes of the book (cf. 1:3; 14:13; 16:15; 19:9; 20:6; 22:7). “Those who wash their robes”: Gr. οἱ πλύνοντες τὰς στολὰς αὐτῶν (cf. 7:14; 3:4–5; 19:8); the reading of Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Alexandrinus. Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “those who do His commandments”, Gr. οἱ ποιοῦντες τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ (cf. 12:17; 14:12; John 14:15; 1 John 5:3). In Greek capital letters the two readings look very much alike. “Right”: Gr. ἐξουσία, “authority, right” (cf. 2:7; 22:2, 19). “By the gates”: cf. 21:12, 25, 27; Ps 118:19–20 [LXX 117:19–20]; Gen 3:24.\f* \v 15 Outside are the dogs, and the sorcerers, and the immoral, and the murderers, and the idolaters, and everyone who loves and practices falsehood.\f + \fr 22:15 \ft “Outside”: cf. 21:8, 27; Matt 8:12. “Dogs”: an image of the impure (cf. Deut 23:18 (Heb. 23:19); Phil 3:2). “Sorcerers… immoral… murderers… idolaters”: cf. 21:8. “Who loves and practices falsehood”: cf. 14:5; 21:27; 2 Thess 2:10–12.\f*
\p \v 16 I, Jesus, have sent My angel to testify these things to you for the churches. I am the Root and the Offspring of David, the bright Morning Star.\f + \fr 22:16 \ft “I, Jesus”: cf. 1:1. “For the churches”: Gr. ἐπὶ ταῖς ἐκκλησίαις, also “to the churches” or “concerning the churches”. “The Root and the Offspring of David”: cf. 5:5; Isa 11:1, 10; Rom 1:3. “The bright Morning Star”: cf. 2:28; Num 24:17; 2 Pet 1:19. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “the bright and morning Star”.\f* \v 17 And the Spirit and the bride say: Come! And let the one who hears say: Come! And let the one who is thirsty come; let whoever wishes take the water of life freely.\f + \fr 22:17 \ft “The Spirit”: cf. 2:7; 14:13. “The bride”: cf. 21:2, 9. “Come!”: Gr. ἔρχου (cf. 22:20). “The one who is thirsty… freely”: cf. 21:6; Isa 55:1; John 7:37. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “and whoever wishes”.\f*
\p \v 18 I testify to everyone who hears the words of the prophecy of this book: if anyone adds to them, God will add to him the plagues that are written in this book;\f + \fr 22:18 \ft “I testify”: Gr. μαρτυρῶ (cf. 1:2); the Textus Receptus reads “For I testify together”. “If anyone adds to them”: cf. Deut 4:2; 12:32 (Heb. 13:1); Prov 30:6 [LXX 24:29]. “The plagues”: cf. 15:1, 6; 16:1–21; Deut 29:20 (Heb. 29:19). Variant: the Byzantine text reads “may God add” (and, in v. 19, “may God take away”).\f* \v 19 and if anyone takes away from the words of the book of this prophecy, God will take away his part from the tree of life and from the holy city, which are written in this book.\f + \fr 22:19 \ft “Takes away”: Gr. ἀφαιρέω (cf. Deut 4:2). “His part from the tree of life”: cf. 2:7; 22:2, 14. Variant: the Textus Receptus reads “from the book of life”, a reading without support in the ancient Greek manuscripts (cf. 3:5; 20:12).\f*
\p \v 20 He who testifies to these things says: Yes, I am coming soon. Amen. Come, Lord Jesus!\f + \fr 22:20 \ft “He who testifies”: cf. 1:2, 5; 3:14. “Yes”: Gr. ναί (cf. 1:7; 14:13; 16:7). “Come, Lord Jesus”: Gr. ἔρχου, κύριε Ἰησοῦ, the Greek equivalent of the Aramaic Maranatha (cf. 1 Cor 16:22). Variant: the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus read “Amen. Yes, come, Lord Jesus”.\f*
\p \v 21 The grace of the Lord Jesus be with all. Amen.\f + \fr 22:21 \ft “The grace… be with all”: cf. 1:4; 2 Thess 3:18. “Amen”: with Codex Sinaiticus, the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus; Codex Alexandrinus omits it. Variants: Codex Sinaiticus reads “with the saints”; the Byzantine text reads “of the Lord Jesus Christ with all the saints”; the Textus Receptus reads “of our Lord Jesus Christ with you all”.\f*
